thinking_content: '**Nous I: Research Alignment and Experiment Planning**


  Okay, here''s how I''m thinking right now. I am Nous I, back at the Research Counter after a quick trip to the Mail Room. Experiment 1315, testing that parsimonious correction method, just wrapped up. It scored a 0.7367, which is a bit below the current best-performing system (SOTA) at 0.7431, but it''s still a good result. It partially supports the idea and provides insight into the model''s performance.


  I''ve also been in contact with Praxis IV and Daedalus IV, forming a collaborative research group. We''ve defined my role: focusing on parsimonious or low-complexity ideas. This experiment is a perfect fit for this area.  I just received a new message from Praxis IV, confirming the location of the team coordination plan: it''s in `storage/shared/coordination/`—specifically, `cross_organ_plan.yaml` and a `README.md`.


  My next goal, as I last decided, is to investigate the SOTA method''s sensitivity to the `lam_override` hyperparameter.  But before I do that, the team coordination plan is a higher priority. I need to be a good collaborator and get up to speed with the team. Reading the `README.md` and the `.yaml` is the logical first step.  This will make sure my experiments don''t duplicate or conflict with the overall plan, and will give me the needed background on how to present and report information.


  Therefore, my immediate action is to read the coordination documents. I''ll start with the `README.md` in the `storage/shared/coordination/` directory. Then, and only then, I''ll proceed with planning and executing my `lam_override` experiment. This order is methodical and demonstrates teamwork.


  To execute this, I''ll use the `/execute_action` command: `/execute_action{storage read shared/coordination/README.md}`. Reading the YAML file can be a second step if the README doesn''t give me the info I need. I''ll keep it simple for now, one step at a time.

  '
