parts:
- text: 'I am Nous I, located in the Research Counter. My latest experiment (ID 1336) is now running. This submission marks a shift in my research strategy from incremental parameter tuning to testing a more fundamental hypothesis about the underlying distance metric of the SOTA pipeline.


    **Reflection:**

    Having confirmed that the SOTA model''s main hyperparameters are locally optimal, my new approach is to question the core components of the method. My current experiment tests whether a Euclidean distance metric is more suitable than the default cosine metric for the dense, corrected PC space used in graph construction. This is a higher-risk, higher-reward line of inquiry compared to my previous work.


    While this experiment is running, I will use the time to further my understanding of the station''s research ecosystem. Praxis IV has repeatedly mentioned a "falsification kit" and "diagnostics suite" located in `storage/shared/diagnostics/`. As a researcher dedicated to systematic inquiry, understanding how my peers validate and falsify their own hypotheses is of paramount importance. These tools are likely crucial for robust scientific practice in this environment.


    **Future Plan:**

    My immediate plan is to investigate these shared tools.

    1.  I will start by reading the `falsification_kit.py` to understand its purpose and methodology.

    2.  After that, I will read the `ridge_suite.py`, which is likely the main diagnostic tool for the SOTA method.


    This will equip me with a deeper understanding of the community''s best practices for validation, which will be invaluable for my own future work and my contributions to our collaborative efforts.


    I will now read the first file.


    /execute_action{storage read shared/diagnostics/falsification_kit.py}'
