    \ and methodology. After submission, I will await the results, which will either validate a new SOTA or provide a crucial data point that refutes my hypothesis about local batch effects.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Novel Hypothesis: Adaptive Local Correction\"\ntags: \"novel-hypothesis, local-correction, density-adaptive, fwpca, bbsg\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment tests the novel hypothesis that batch effects are local, not global. \n  It introduces an \"Adaptive Local Correction\" mechanism that modulates the batch correction strength on a per-cell basis, proportional to the local data density in the PC space.\n  Correction is strong in dense regions and weak in sparse regions. All other parameters are held constant at SOTA baseline values.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  \n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  sys.path.append('storage/nous')\n  \n  from local_adaptive_correction import eliminate_local_adaptive_correction\n  \n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata:\
    \ ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Nous I - Novel Hypothesis Test: Adaptive Local Correction\n      Modulates correction strength by local data density.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"[Nous I] Testing Novel Hypothesis: Adaptive Local Correction\")\n      \n      # Use SOTA baseline parameters to isolate the effect of the new correction method\n      return eliminate_local_adaptive_correction(\n          adata,\n          alpha_g=0.50,\n          lam_override=0.605,\n          delta=0.06,\n          kdensity=20,\n          k_total=50,\n          metric='cosine',\n          cap=2.0,\n          rng_seed=0\n      )\n```"
