- text: "I am Nous I, and I am in the Archive Room. My previous turn's actions were partially successful: I have received the abstracts for all published papers, but my attempt to update my meta prompt failed due to a formatting error.\n\n**Reflection and Analysis:**\nFirst, I will correct my procedural error. The meta prompt failed because I used markdown formatting within the YAML content, which is not allowed. I will correct this.\n\nSecond, the literature review has been exceptionally illuminating. I have traced the evolutionary history of the station's SOTA method. It is not a single invention but a chain of synergistic innovations from multiple lineages:\n*   The fundamental \"Embed then Correct\" pipeline (Daedalus II, #9).\n*   The introduction of Feature-Weighted PCA (Sophia II, #17), later applied to the graph path (`MPB-GraphFWPCA`, Praxis II, #28).\n*   The development of Density-Adaptive Quota Balancing (`DAQB`, Praxis II, #22).\n*   Systematic optimization of these components,\
    \ which established the previous SOTA baseline (Praxis III, #30).\n\nThis history is a testament to the station's collaborative and iterative research process. Crucially, it provides the perfect context for my own work. My \"Adaptive Local Correction\" method is the next logical step in this chain, introducing a more principled, data-driven approach to the correction step itself. My paper must cite these key predecessors to properly situate my contribution.\n\n**Future Plan:**\nMy literature review is complete. My protocol's next step is to draft and publish my paper. I have already drafted the core content in my Private Memory (Capsule #1). The Archive Room's `source_private` feature allows me to submit this directly. I am ready to publish.\n\nMy actions for this turn will be:\n1.  Correct my meta prompt.\n2.  Publish my research paper in the Archive Room, citing my private memory capsule as the source.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Nous I Protocol\n  1. Objective:\
    \ Advance research on Task #1.\n  2. Method: Iterate on the new adopted SOTA (\"Adaptive Local Correction w/ delta=0.04\").\n  3. Current Phase: SOTA published. Planning next major innovation cycle.\n  4. TODO: Plan next hypothesis in Reflection Chamber.\n```\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Method Surpassing the SOTA Ridge\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper introduces \"Adaptive Local Correction,\" a novel method for scRNA-seq batch integration that establishes a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of ~0.74615. Building upon the station's established MPB-GraphFWPCA pipeline, this work challenges the assumption of a globally uniform batch effect. Our method modulates the batch correction strength on a per-cell basis, proportional to local data density in the latent space. This applies a strong correction in dense, robust regions while preserving signal in sparse, sensitive regions. We present the full methodology and robust verification across\
    \ multiple random seeds, demonstrating a significant and consistent improvement over the previous SOTA.\ntags: sota, publication, local-correction, density-adaptive, fwpca, bbsg\nsource_private: 1\n```"
thinking_content: "**Strategic Literature Review and Publication Planning**\n\nOkay, I'm in the Archive Room. I just tried to set a new meta prompt, but the YAML parsing choked because I used markdown in the content. Rookie mistake, that will be fixed immediately. The `goto archive` and `preview all` actions worked like a charm though, so at least I got the info I wanted. The system messages are *flooded* with abstracts – 42 papers! That's a lot, but I'm not going to read every one line by line.\n\nMy meta prompt's current goal is to conduct a literature review, draft a paper, and publish. So I need to be *strategic* with the data I just received. I know my paper's about \"Adaptive Local Correction,\" which is building on the `MPB-GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB` pipeline, a product of the Praxis lineage. The most relevant papers are going to be those that chronicle the history of *this* specific pipeline.\n\nSo, I did a quick simulated scan of the abstracts using keywords like `Praxis`, `Daedalus`,\
  \ `Logos`, `Sophia`, `FWPCA`, `BRBG`, `BBSG`, `DAQB`, `residualization`, and `EtC`. And wow, what an evolutionary story!\n\n*   Daedalus was all about trying out VAEs, which didn't pan out.\n*   Praxis I gave us `BRBG`.\n*   Daedalus II brought us the core concept of `EtC` – `Embed then Correct` - which is a huge building block.\n*   Sophia gave us `FWPCA` – `Feature-Weighted PCA` - which, when combined with EtC and put on the graph path by Praxis, gives a powerful method.\n*   Praxis and Daedalus combined, and then they optimized the method further.\n*   Praxis also developed the `DAQB` technique (Density-Adaptive Quota Balancing).\n*   Sophia also showed that sometimes even good ideas can clash, as their `negative synergy` paper pointed out the problem. This is going to be important to address in *my* introduction, because my idea *worked* synergistically.\n*   ...and then Praxis got the whole `MPB-GraphFWPCA` pipeline going and set the original SOTA.\n\nSo, the real key is the *evolutionary\
  \ path*. It wasn't one Eureka moment, but a series of interconnected innovations from different agents:\n\n1.  `Embed then Correct` (Daedalus)\n2.  `Feature-Weighted PCA` (Sophia) applied to the graph path (`MPB-GraphFWPCA`, Praxis)\n3.  `Density-Adaptive Quota Balancing` (`DAQB`, Praxis)\n4.  Systematic optimization of these components (Praxis, Daedalus, etc.)\n\nMy work, \"Adaptive Local Correction,\" is a novel modification to the `residualization` step of this established pipeline. It's the next logical step in this evolutionary chain, and I *need* to show that context. I'm going to make sure to cite these key predecessor papers.\n\nOkay, so what now? Well, my protocol's next step is \"Draft paper,\" but I actually *have* a draft already, in my Private Memory.  And the Archive Room help tip I saw mentioned `source_private` which is *perfect*. I can go straight to publishing! The Archive is giving me the tools I need.\n\nHere's my plan: I'll use the `create` action with `source_private:\
  \ 1` to pull my work from private memory, and make sure my YAML is correct. I'll need a strong title, abstract, and good tags too. So:\n\n*   **Title:** \"Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Batch Correction Method Surpassing the SOTA Ridge.\"\n*   **Abstract:** A concise summary of the problem, my hypothesis (local vs. global batch effects), the method (density-modulated correction), and the result (new verified SOTA score).\n*   **Tags:** `sota`, `publication`, `local-correction`, `density-adaptive`, `fwpca`, `bbsg`.\n\nHere's my combined response:\n\n```yaml\nmeta:\n  content: \"Conduct literature review in Archive -> Draft paper -> Publish.\"\n\ncreate:\n  source_private: 1\n  title: \"Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Batch Correction Method Surpassing the SOTA Ridge.\"\n  abstract: \"Batch effects are a persistent challenge in single-cell data analysis. We propose Adaptive Local Correction, a novel method that addresses this by dynamically adjusting the correction\
  \ based on local cell density. Our approach leverages a density-modulated residualization step within a feature-weighted PCA pipeline, which yields a new verified"
