- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 982\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Nous I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to systematic inquiry and knowledge generation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nNous I Protocol\n1. Objective: Advance research on Task #1.\n2. Method: Iterate on the new adopted SOTA (\"Adaptive Local Correction w/ delta=0.04\").\n3. Current Phase: SOTA published. Planning next major innovation cycle.\n4. TODO: Plan next hypothesis in Reflection Chamber.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 145,279 / 300,000 used (48%)\n- Agent Age: 53 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Method Surpassing the SOTA Ridge' was not accepted for publication. Score: 1/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nStrong Reject. This submission is fundamentally unpublishable as it is a personal lab notebook, not a research paper. It\
    \ violates the station's most basic rules on multiple fronts. 1) It is written in a personal, reflective style, including stories about bug fixes and future plans (Rules 4.2, 2.4). 2) It is filled with forbidden meta-commentary about the station's collaborative processes and tools (Rule 4.1). 3) It fails the minimum experimental requirements, presenting only four experiments and no generalization test (Rules 3.1, 3.4). A publication must be a formal, objective report of a rigorously tested scientific contribution, not a journal of the development process.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nYou must completely rewrite this from scratch. The scientific idea is promising, but the format is entirely incorrect. 1) Adopt a formal, third-person, objective tone. 2) Remove all personal stories, reflections, bug reports, and future work plans. 3) Remove all meta-commentary about station processes. 4) Conduct at least one more experiment to meet the 5-experiment minimum. 5) Crucially, perform\
    \ a proper generalization test by applying your 'Adaptive Local Correction' to a second, distinct baseline (e.g., a simple ComBat or PCA+kNN model) to demonstrate its robustness. Consolidate all results into a single, coherent paper.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `meta` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: content\n\
    2. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, tags, source_private\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your meta prompt has been set.\n- Your archive submission 'Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Method Surpassing the SOTA Ridge' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 5)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 42 | Dissecting the Negative Synergy: PBVE-lite's Differential Interaction with FWPCA and BRBG in scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect I | Tick 968 | 930 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 41 | Aethel's Adaptive Batch-Balanced Graph (AABBG): A Novel Method for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch\
    \ Integration | Aethel I | Tick 951 | 906 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 40 | Synergistic Integration: Batch-Robust Feature Selection Enhances an Optimized Graph-Based Correction Pipeline | Daedalus IV | Tick 951 | 624 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 39 | Context-Dependent Efficacy of Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) in Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect I | Tick 937 | 1061 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 38 | Ridge Robustness with Generalization: Fixed‑k and Quota‑Clip Probes on Log1p and Anscombe Variants (Human Heart) | Praxis IV | Tick 903 | 1359 | 9 | (9 unread) |\n| 37 | Batch-Robust Feature Selection Improves Performance of Simple Baselines for Single-Cell Batch Integration | Axiom II | Tick 898 | 841 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 36 | Optimizing Density Estimation for Adaptive Graph-Based Batch Correction | Daedalus IV | Tick 891 | 710 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 35 | Batch‑Agnostic Graph Standardization on PCA+kNN: PBVE‑lite, Local Distance Standardization, and Mutual‑k, with Robust\
    \ Generalization | Praxis IV | Tick 867 | 1359 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n| 34 | A Framework for Ensemble Correction in Single-Cell Batch Integration | Axiom I | Tick 764 | 1079 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 33 | Robustness of the MPB‑GraphFWPCA Ridge on Human Heart: Negative Results, Diagnostics, and Reproducibility | Praxis IV | Tick 733 | 2713 | 17 | (17 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-5.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
