tick: 983
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Nous I, located in the Research Counter. I have successfully created the script `nous/generalization_test_combat.py` to conduct the generalization experiment required by the publication reviewers.\n\n**Reflection:**\nThe rejection of my paper was a necessary lesson. Science demands rigor, and a key component of that is demonstrating that a new method is not just a tweak that works on one specific, complex model but a fundamental improvement that can generalize. My new script applies the core idea of \"Adaptive Local Correction\" to the much simpler ComBat baseline. This is a very different application: instead of correcting a low-dimensional PC space, I am modulating the correction of the high-dimensional gene expression matrix itself.\n\nI also note the recent activity from other agents. The station is a dynamic environment, and maintaining a state-of-the-art score requires continuous innovation. My current task is to prove the robustness of my discovery, which is a prerequisite\
    \ for any future work.\n\n**Future Plan:**\nMy `TODO` is clear. I have created the script; now I must run it. I will submit this experiment immediately. A positive result—an improvement over the standard ComBat baseline (ID 2, score ~0.5895)—will provide the crucial evidence of generalizability that I need for my paper.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Generalization Test: Adaptive Local Correction on ComBat\"\ntags: \"generalization, local-correction, combat, publication-requirement\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment is a generalization test of my \"Adaptive Local Correction\" method, as required by publication reviewers.\n  The method is applied to the simple ComBat baseline (ID 2) to demonstrate its robustness and general applicability beyond the complex SOTA pipeline.\n  This test modulates the Combat correction on the gene expression matrix based on local density in a temporary PC space.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import numpy as np\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import\
    \ anndata as ad\n  from scipy.sparse import issparse\n  from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors\n\n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  sys.path.append('storage/nous')\n  from local_adaptive_correction import _fw_weight_by_var # Re-use feature weighting\n\n  # This is a simplified, standalone version of the Combat logic\n  # from the system baseline, adapted to integrate the local correction.\n  def combat_local_correction(adata: ad.AnnData, kdensity: int = 20):\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n      # --- Start of Novelty: Local Correction applied to Expression Matrix ---\n      # Unlike the SOTA, this operates on the high-dimensional gene space.\n      # We use a PCA embedding *only* to calculate the density factor.\n      print(\"  Creating temporary PCA for density estimation...\")\n      temp_pca = sc.pp.pca(adata, n_comps=50, copy=True)\n      Zg = temp_pca.obsm['X_pca']\n\n      print(\"  Calculating local density for adaptive\
    \ correction...\")\n      nn_density = NearestNeighbors(n_neighbors=kdensity + 1, metric='cosine', algorithm='brute')\n      nn_density.fit(Zg)\n      d_k, _ = nn_density.kneighbors(Zg, return_distance=True)\n      d_k = d_k[:, -1]\n      d_min, d_max = float(np.min(d_k)), float(np.max(d_k))\n      normalized_dist = (d_k - d_min) / (d_max - d_min + 1e-8)\n      density_factor = 1.0 - normalized_dist\n\n      if issparse(adata.X):\n          data = adata.X.toarray()\n      else:\n          data = adata.X.copy()\n      data_T = data.T # Genes x Samples\n\n      # --- Simplified Combat Logic ---\n      batch_labels = adata.obs['batch'].values\n      unique_batches, batch_counts = np.unique(batch_labels, return_counts=True)\n      n_batches = len(unique_batches)\n      n_genes, n_samples = data_T.shape\n      \n      batch_design = np.eye(n_batches)[np.searchsorted(unique_batches, batch_labels)]\n      \n      # Estimate batch effects (gamma_hat)\n      B_hat = np.linalg.inv(batch_design.T\
    \ @ batch_design) @ batch_design.T\n      gamma_hat = B_hat @ data_T.T\n\n      # Apply adaptive correction to the estimated batch effects\n      # Modulate the effect for each cell, then average back to a per-batch effect\n      corrected_gamma = np.zeros_like(gamma_hat)\n      for i, batch in enumerate(unique_batches):\n          batch_mask = (batch_labels == batch)\n          # Apply the per-cell density factor to the batch effect vector\n          cell_specific_effect = gamma_hat[i, :][np.newaxis, :] * density_factor[batch_mask, np.newaxis]\n          # Average the modulated effects to get the new batch effect\n          corrected_gamma[i, :] = np.mean(cell_specific_effect, axis=0)\n      \n      # Correct the data\n      bayesdata_T = data_T.copy()\n      for i, batch in enumerate(unique_batches):\n          batch_mask = (batch_labels == batch)\n          bayesdata_T[:, batch_mask] -= corrected_gamma[i, :][:, np.newaxis]\n\n      # Return a new AnnData with corrected expression\
    \ matrix for PCA\n      corrected_adata = ad.AnnData(bayesdata_T.T)\n      sc.pp.pca(corrected_adata, n_comps=50)\n      # Create a valid output AnnData object\n      output = ad.AnnData(obs=adata.obs[[]], var=adata.var[[]])\n      output.obsm['X_emb'] = corrected_adata.obsm['X_pca']\n      return output\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      print(\"[Nous I] Generalization Test: Adaptive Local Correction on ComBat Baseline.\")\n      return combat_local_correction(adata)\n```"
