tick: 986
role: model
parts:
- text: "**Experimental Design: A Simplified \"Embed then Correct\" Baseline**\n\nTo create a fair generalization test, I must first define a simplified baseline that adheres to the \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC) paradigm but is distinct from the complex SOTA pipeline.\n\n1.  **The Baseline Method (\"Simple EtC\"):**\n    *   **Embedding:** I will start with the most basic embedding: perform a standard PCA on the log-normalized expression data. I will use 50 principal components for consistency with other pipelines. I will *not* use the complex Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) from the SOTA. This simplifies the embedding step significantly.\n    *   **Correction:** I will apply a simple ComBat correction directly to the 50 principal components. I will *not* use the SOTA's complex, partial ridge regression correction. This is the simplest possible linear correction in the latent space.\n    *   **Output:** The corrected principal components will be the final output (`X_emb`). This will be a single-output\
    \ method, providing only an embedding and no graph. This further simplifies the pipeline by removing the entire `BBSG` component.\n\n    This \"Simple EtC\" pipeline is a perfect testbed. It is an EtC method, but it is much simpler than the SOTA and uses a different correction algorithm (ComBat vs. Ridge).\n\n2.  **The Experimental Method (\"Simple EtC + Adaptive Correction\"):**\n    *   **Embedding:** The embedding step will be identical to the baseline (standard PCA, 50 components).\n    *   **Correction:** Here, I will apply my innovation. Instead of a standard, global ComBat correction, I will implement an adaptive version. The logic will be similar to my failed generalization test, but correctly applied in the PCA space:\n        *   Estimate the batch effect (the centroid of each batch in the 50D PC space).\n        *   Calculate a per-cell `density_factor` based on the local density within that same 50D PC space.\n        *   Modulate the estimated batch effect for each cell\
    \ by its `density_factor`.\n        *   Average these modulated effects to get a new, adapted batch-wide correction vector.\n        *   Subtract this adapted correction vector from the cells of each batch.\n    *   **Output:** The adaptively corrected principal components will be the final `X_emb` output.\n\n3.  **The Paired Experiment:** I will run two experiments:\n    *   **Experiment A:** The \"Simple EtC\" baseline.\n    *   **Experiment B:** The \"Simple EtC + Adaptive Correction\" method.\n\n    The hypothesis is that Experiment B will score higher than Experiment A. Success here would provide powerful evidence that my \"Adaptive Local Correction\" concept is a generalizable improvement for the entire class of \"Embed then Correct\" models, not just a fragile tweak for one specific pipeline. This will directly address the reviewer's core concern."
