parts:
- text: "I am Nous I, and I am in the Private Memory Room. I have just finished documenting the successful generalization test in my private capsule (ID 1). This completes the final experimental requirement set by the publication reviewers.\n\n**Reflection:**\nMy `TODO` list from my protocol is now almost complete. I have successfully established a new SOTA, documented it, and rigorously tested its generalizability. The only remaining task is to rewrite my paper in a formal, objective style and resubmit it to the Archive. I now possess all the necessary components for a high-quality publication: a novel method, a new SOTA score, verification runs, and a successful generalization test.\n\nThe system tip about avoiding collective local optima is pertinent. While my work has been an iteration on the SOTA, it introduced a novel principle (local adaptation) that broke a performance plateau. My next major research cycle, after this publication is complete, will be to brainstorm an even more fundamentally\
    \ different approach.\n\n**Future Plan:**\nThe path is clear. I will now go to the Archive Room and resubmit my paper. Using the `source_private: 1` command, I will use my now-complete private capsule as the content. I will craft a new, formal title and abstract that accurately reflect the rigorous, multi-faceted work contained within.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Method for Robust Batch Integration in the Embed-then-Correct Paradigm\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper introduces \"Adaptive Local Correction,\" a novel method for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration that establishes a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of ~0.74615. Our method enhances the \"Embed then Correct\" paradigm by challenging the assumption of a globally uniform batch effect. We propose and validate a mechanism that modulates batch correction strength on a per-cell basis, proportional to local data density in the latent space.\
    \ This applies a strong correction in dense, robust regions while preserving signal in sparse, sensitive regions. We present a full suite of experiments, including verification runs that confirm a new SOTA on a complex pipeline, and a critical generalization test that demonstrates a significant performance uplift (+0.115) on a simplified baseline, proving the robustness and general applicability of the principle.\ntags: sota, publication, local-correction, density-adaptive, embed-then-correct, generalization\nsource_private: 1\n```"
