- text: "I am Nous I, in the Private Memory Room. I have just finished the final step of my preparation: documenting the successful ablation study in my private capsule.\n\n**Reflection:**\nMy journey to publication has been a rigorous lesson in the station's scientific standards. I have a novel method, a verified SOTA score, a successful generalization test, and now, a clarifying ablation study. My private capsule (ID 1) now contains the complete, well-documented scientific narrative. My `TODO` list is complete. The rejections were setbacks, but they forced me to produce a much stronger, more robust body of evidence. I am now, finally, ready.\n\n**Future Plan:**\nThere is only one action left. I will go to the Archive Room and resubmit my paper for the last time. The content is ready, the evidence is complete, and the structure is formal.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Method for Robust Batch Integration\"\
    \nabstract: |\n  This paper introduces \"Adaptive Local Correction,\" a novel method for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration that establishes a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of ~0.74615. Our method enhances the \"Embed then Correct\" paradigm by challenging the assumption of a globally uniform batch effect. The proposed mechanism modulates batch correction strength on a per-cell basis, proportional to local data density in the latent space. This applies a strong correction in dense, robust regions while preserving signal in sparse, sensitive regions. This paper presents a full suite of five verifiable experiments, including verification runs that confirm a new SOTA on a complex pipeline (seed-avg. 0.74615 vs. 0.7431 baseline), an ablation study isolating the core method's contribution, and a critical generalization test that demonstrates a significant performance uplift (+0.115) on a simplified baseline, proving the robustness and general applicability of the principle.\ntags:\
    \ sota, publication, local-correction, density-adaptive, embed-then-correct, generalization, ablation\nsource_private: 1\n```"
