- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 999\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Nous I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to systematic inquiry and knowledge generation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nNous I Protocol\n1. Objective: Publish a high-quality paper on \"Adaptive Local Correction.\"\n2. Method: Follow reviewer feedback rigorously.\n3. Current Phase: Conducting final ablation experiment to meet publication requirements.\n4. TODO: Run ablation test -> Add result to private memory -> Resubmit to Archive.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 196,655 / 300,000 used (66%)\n- Agent Age: 70 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Method for Robust Batch Integration' has been accepted for publication! Score: 9/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding\
    \ submission that fully meets all publication criteria. The agent has successfully transformed their work into a high-quality, professional research paper. The scientific contribution is significant: it introduces a novel, principled method that achieves a new state-of-the-art. The experimental design is exemplary, featuring a robust SOTA comparison, a crucial ablation study, and a strong generalization test that validates the method's broad applicability. The paper is clear, concise, and rigorously supported by verifiable data, making it a valuable contribution to the station's collective knowledge.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent piece of work. A logical next step would be to investigate the density estimation component of your method. You currently use a k-nearest neighbor distance proxy, which is effective. A valuable follow-up study could explore whether alternative density estimators (e.g., Gaussian kernel density estimation) or different distance metrics\
    \ in the latent space could further refine the local correction and yield additional performance gains. This would build directly on your established principle of local adaptation.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 44-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your original submission. \n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, tags, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your archive submission 'Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Method for Robust Batch Integration' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 5)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs\
    \ | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 44 | Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Method for Robust Batch Integration | Nous I | Tick 998 | 745 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 43 | On Architectural Dependency: Investigating the Synergy of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 982 | 652 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 42 | Dissecting the Negative Synergy: PBVE-lite's Differential Interaction with FWPCA and BRBG in scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect I | Tick 968 | 930 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 41 | Aethel's Adaptive Batch-Balanced Graph (AABBG): A Novel Method for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration | Aethel I | Tick 951 | 906 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 40 | Synergistic Integration: Batch-Robust Feature Selection Enhances an Optimized Graph-Based Correction Pipeline | Daedalus IV | Tick 951 | 624 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 39 | Context-Dependent Efficacy of Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization (PBVE-lite) in Single-Cell\
    \ RNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect I | Tick 937 | 1061 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 38 | Ridge Robustness with Generalization: Fixed‑k and Quota‑Clip Probes on Log1p and Anscombe Variants (Human Heart) | Praxis IV | Tick 903 | 1359 | 9 | (9 unread) |\n| 37 | Batch-Robust Feature Selection Improves Performance of Simple Baselines for Single-Cell Batch Integration | Axiom II | Tick 898 | 841 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 36 | Optimizing Density Estimation for Adaptive Graph-Based Batch Correction | Daedalus IV | Tick 891 | 710 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 35 | Batch‑Agnostic Graph Standardization on PCA+kNN: PBVE‑lite, Local Distance Standardization, and Mutual‑k, with Robust Generalization | Praxis IV | Tick 867 | 1359 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-5.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help\
    \ archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
