tick: 1001
role: model
parts:
- text: "**Implementation and Experimental Plan**\n\nTo test this hypothesis, I will create a new experiment that modifies my best-performing \"Adaptive Local Correction\" pipeline.\n\n1.  **Create New Core Module:** I will write a new file, `nous/local_adaptive_correction_kde.py`. This will contain a new function, `eliminate_alc_kde`, which will be a direct fork of my now-published `eliminate_local_adaptive_correction` function. This keeps my successful SOTA method intact and creates a clean environment for this new experiment.\n\n2.  **Modify the Density Estimation Step:** The central change will be to replace the `sklearn.neighbors.NearestNeighbors` block with a `scipy.stats.gaussian_kde` implementation.\n    *   I will instantiate the `gaussian_kde` object using the pre-correction PC space (`Zg`, transposed to match Scipy's expected shape of `dims x n_points`). A key advantage of this Scipy implementation is its automatic bandwidth estimation (using Scott's or Silverman's rule), which\
    \ provides a principled default and avoids a complex, multi-dimensional hyperparameter search for the initial test.\n    *   I will then evaluate the KDE on each data point (cell) to get its density estimate.\n    *   These density values will be normalized to a [0, 1] range to produce the new `density_factor`. This ensures the scale of the modulation is comparable to my previous k-NN based method.\n\n3.  **Acknowledge Computational Risk:** Kernel Density Estimation is computationally more intensive than a k-NN search, especially in a 50-dimensional space. There is a significant risk that this implementation might exceed the 30-minute execution timeout. This experiment will therefore also serve as a feasibility test for using KDE in this context.\n\n4.  **Design the Submission:** I will submit a single experiment. The script will call my new `eliminate_alc_kde` function. To ensure a direct and fair comparison, all other hyperparameters will be set to the optimal values from my best-performing\
    \ run (ID 1351/1355):\n    *   `alpha_g=0.50`\n    *   `lam_override=0.605`\n    *   `delta=0.04` (the optimized value from my interaction probe)\n    *   `k_total=50`\n\nThis setup will isolate the effect of the density estimation method. The baseline for comparison will be my published SOTA score of ~0.74615. Any significant deviation will be directly attributable to the change from k-NN to KDE."
thinking_content: '**Implementing Adaptive Local Correction with KDE**


  Okay, so I''m building on the previous discussion. The goal is to figure out *how* to implement this "Adaptive Local Correction with KDE" (ALC-KDE) thing. The "what" and "why" are already sorted, now it''s down to the nuts and bolts.


  First, the code. I''ll make a new file, `nous/local_adaptive_correction_kde.py`. Keeps things organized and clean. Standard practice.


  The main thing is going to be the KDE part. KDE has a bandwidth parameter, which is a bit of a headache. I can''t just guess, and grid searching for the optimal bandwidth on 20,000 cells sounds brutal. But, wait, `scipy.stats.gaussian_kde` has automatic bandwidth selection (Scott''s rule, which is a good default). We''ve got `scipy` in the libraries, so I can use that, no need for complex grid searches. This is great; keeps things simple and avoids over-tuning.


  Here''s how it''ll work: I''ll `fit` `gaussian_kde` on the principal components (`Zg`), then use `evaluate` (or just call it directly) to get the density at each point. Then I''ll normalize these scores into the density factor, so it works similarly to the old method. Easy.


  Now, a big "but": KDE can be slow. I''m a bit worried about this. Especially on a dataset this size. I''ll need to keep an eye on performance. If it''s too slow, I might need to consider a subset of the data for fitting, but let''s try the full dataset first. I need to make sure to point this out as a potential problem.


  The experiment itself is straightforward. This is a direct test against my best previous method, which is "Adaptive Local Correction" with `delta=0.04` and `kdensity=20` (from ID 1351/1355). That''s the baseline. The new "ALC-KDE" submission will use the same pipeline but swap the k-NN density part for the KDE part. Crucially, I''ll keep all the other parameters (`alpha_g`, `lambda`, `delta`, `k_total`) exactly the same as the baseline. This is a head-to-head comparison.


  So, one new submission, calling this new `local_adaptive_correction_kde.py` function. It''ll use those optimal parameters from the previous run: `alpha_g=0.50`, `lam_override=0.605`, `delta=0.04`, `k_total=50`. This is the core of the plan.


  This is a solid plan. Specific. Achievable. It''s got the right level of detail and acknowledges the potential risks. I think it''ll work.

  '
