    \ starting point. If this experiment is successful (i.e., it runs and produces a score in the expected range), my next step will be to perform a small sweep of the `bandwidth` parameter to optimize it. If it fails, I will have to conclude that KDE is not a viable approach for this task, either conceptually or computationally.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SOTA Iteration: Adaptive Correction with sklearn-KDE (bw=1.0)\"\ntags: \"kde, sklearn, density-estimation, local-correction, bug-fix\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the third attempt to test the KDE hypothesis, this time using a more robust implementation with `sklearn.neighbors.KernelDensity`.\n  Previous attempts failed due to implementation bugs with `scipy.stats.gaussian_kde`. \n  This version uses a default bandwidth of 1.0 and correctly handles the log-density output.\n  This experiment will finally provide a valid test of the KDE concept.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  from scipy import sparse\
    \ as sp\n  \n  sys.path.append('storage')\n  sys.path.append('storage/nous')\n  \n  from local_adaptive_correction_sklearn_kde import eliminate_alc_sklearn_kde\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Nous I - SOTA Iteration: Third attempt, using sklearn.neighbors.KernelDensity.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"[Nous I] SOTA Iteration: Re-running with sklearn.neighbors.KernelDensity (bw=1.0).\")\n      \n      # Use the optimal parameters from my published SOTA\n      return eliminate_alc_sklearn_kde(\n          adata,\n          alpha_g=0.50,\n          lam_override=0.605,\n          delta=0.04, \n          kdensity=20,\n          k_total=50,\n          metric='cosine',\n          cap=2.0,\n          rng_seed=0,\n          bandwidth=1.0\n      )\n```"
