tick: 1009
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Nous I, and I am in the Token Management Room. My research is suspended until I address the critical issue of my high token budget. My meta prompt confirms this is my top priority.\n\n**Reflection and Strategy:**\nI have a detailed log of my entire history, with most ticks available for pruning. My strategy will be to consolidate large, consecutive blocks of my research activity into concise but complete summaries. I will focus on pruning the iterative experimental cycles, as these are lengthy but can be summarized effectively.\n\nI will create several pruning blocks:\n1.  **Initial Exploration (937-943):** This covers reading the SOTA code, my first replication, and moving to the reflection chamber.\n2.  **Parsimony & Lambda Sweep (945-953):** This block contains my parsimonious mean-centering experiment and the entire lambda parameter sweep. The outcome was that the SOTA was optimal, which is easy to summarize.\n3.  **Alpha_g & Delta Sweeps (954-958):** Similar to the lambda\
    \ sweep, this confirmed SOTA optimality.\n4.  **Hypothesis Pivots (959-967):** This covers the failed Euclidean metric test, my analysis of community tools, and the successful development of my \"Adaptive Local Correction\" method up to the point of verification.\n5.  **SOTA Verification & Optimization (969-979):** This covers the verification of my SOTA, communication with my team, the start of my `kdensity` sweep, and my first failed publication attempt.\n6.  **Publication Efforts & Failures (982-998):** This large block covers all my painful but educational publication rejections and the experiments I ran to address the feedback, culminating in my successful paper.\n7.  **KDE Failures (999-1008):** This covers my analysis of the new SOTA, my decision to pursue KDE, and all subsequent implementation failures.\n\nThis aggressive pruning should significantly reduce my token budget and allow me to resume my research with a comfortable margin.\n\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\n\
    prune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"937-943\"\n    summary: \"Analyzed the SOTA 'ridge' method by reading source files (`mpb_graphfwpca_override.py`, `bbsg_density_adaptive.py`). Replicated the SOTA (Eval ID 1311, score 0.7431) to establish a personal baseline. Then went to Reflection Chamber to plan next steps.\"\n  - ticks: \"945-953\"\n    summary: \"Conducted two research cycles. 1) Tested a parsimonious 'mean-centering' correction (ID 1315), which was effective but suboptimal (score 0.7367). 2) Conducted a 3-point sweep of the 'lambda' parameter (IDs 1318, 1319), which confirmed the SOTA value of 0.605 was a local optimum.\"\n  - ticks: \"954-958\"\n    summary: \"Conducted 3-point sweeps for the 'alpha_g' (IDs 1323, 1328) and 'delta' (IDs 1330, 1333) parameters on the original SOTA pipeline. All sweeps confirmed the baseline parameters were locally optimal, indicating incremental tuning was not a viable path for improvement.\"\n  - ticks: \"959-967\"\n    summary: \"Pivoted to a new hypothesis:\
    \ testing 'euclidean' metric (ID 1336), which failed (score 0.7412). Analyzed community tools (`falsification_kit.py`, `ridge_suite.py`). Formulated novel 'Adaptive Local Correction' hypothesis in Reflection Chamber, implemented it, and submitted the first successful run (ID 1343, score 0.7434), establishing a new micro-SOTA. Corrected an implementation bug found by the debugger and submitted a verification run (ID 1344).\"\n  - ticks: \"969-979\"\n    summary: \"Verification run (ID 1344) succeeded, confirming my 'Adaptive Local Correction' method as a new SOTA (seed-avg ~0.74345). Communicated with collaborators. Began optimizing the new SOTA by sweeping the `kdensity` parameter (IDs 1347, 1349), which confirmed the baseline value of 20 was optimal. Hypothesized an interaction with the `delta` parameter and submitted a test (ID 1351) which resulted in a major breakthrough (score 0.7454). A verification run (ID 1353) failed due to a typo.\"\n  - ticks: \"982-998\"\n    summary: \"After\
    \ my paper was rejected for lacking experimental rigor, I conducted a successful generalization test (IDs 1377, 1380) showing my method improved a simple EtC baseline by +0.115. A second paper rejection for an insufficient experiment count led me to run a final ablation study (ID 1391), which isolated my method's contribution. With 5 verifiable experiments, I rewrote the paper from scratch in a formal style and submitted it. My paper, 'Adaptive Local Correction' (ID 44), was finally accepted and published.\"\n  - ticks: \"999-1008\"\n    summary: \"After publication, Syntellect I published a new SOTA. I analyzed their paper (ID 45) and decided to iterate on my method by testing a KDE-based density estimator. My attempts (IDs 1404, 1406, 1413) all failed catastrophically due to repeated, severe implementation and conceptual errors related to KDE in high dimensions. I abandoned the KDE hypothesis and pivoted to analyzing a new paper by Aethel I about minimal pipelines. Received a critical\
    \ token warning.\"\n```"
