parts:
- text: "I am Nous I, located in the Research Counter. My second, more meticulous replication attempt of Aethel I's \"Minimal SOTA\" pipeline (ID 1429) is now running.\n\n**Reflection:**\nThe debugging process is now in a waiting phase. My hypothesis is that the failure of my first attempt was due to omitting a specific block of code from the original implementation, even though it appeared mathematically redundant. This second attempt corrects that omission. The results of ID 1429 will either validate this hypothesis, allowing me to finally proceed with my optimization plan, or invalidate it, sending me back to a deeper level of debugging.\n\n**Future Plan:**\nWhile I await the results of my replication, I must use the time productively. I cannot submit more experiments until my current one completes. My previous attempt to submit parameterized scripts failed due to a signature mismatch error identified by the debugger. I can use this time to create a robust, reusable, and correctly formatted\
    \ submission script for my planned `delta` sweep. This will allow me to launch the entire sweep quickly and correctly once my baseline is verified.\n\nI will create a single, flexible submission script file that can be easily modified to run each experiment in my sweep.\n\n/execute_action{storage write nous/run_parameterized_minimal_sota.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  \n  # This script provides a clean, reusable template for running the parameterized\n  # minimal SOTA pipeline with different hyperparameter values.\n  \n  # To use this script for an experiment:\n  # 1. Set the DELTA_OVERRIDE value below to the desired value for the experiment.\n  # 2. Submit this script's content directly in the YAML block.\n  \n  # --- EXPERIMENT PARAMETER ---\n  # Set the desired delta value for the experiment here.\n  # Example values from my plan: 0.0, 0.02, 0.04, 0.06, 0.08\n  DELTA_OVERRIDE = 0.0 # Placeholder, will be changed for each submission\n  \n  # --- CORE\
    \ LOGIC ---\n  # This is the parameterized function that will be defined in a separate file\n  # once the replication is successful. For now, this is a placeholder.\n  # The actual implementation will be copied from the successful replication.\n  def parameterized_minimal_sota_fn(adata: ad.AnnData, delta_override: float) -> ad.AnnData:\n      # This function will contain the full logic from the successful \n      # minimal_sota_replication_v2.py, but with `delta` as an argument.\n      # For now, it is empty.\n      pass\n  \n  # --- SUBMISSION ENTRY POINT ---\n  # The evaluator expects a function with this exact name and signature.\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      This wrapper correctly calls the parameterized function with the\n      hyperparameter set above. This avoids the signature mismatch error.\n      \"\"\"\n      # The actual implementation will be copied into the `parameterized_minimal_sota_fn`\n      # and this script\
    \ will call it.\n      # This structure is just a plan. I need to wait for the replication.\n      return None\n```\nMy thinking is still getting ahead of the data. I cannot write the parameterized script until I have a confirmed working baseline to parameterize. The structure I just laid out is sound, but premature.\n\nThe best use of my time is to analyze the leaderboard and my collaborator's activities to prepare for my next steps, assuming the replication succeeds. Praxis V and Daedalus IV are engaged in complex work, while Aethel I is continuing to tune the minimal pipeline. This confirms that my current focus is aligned with productive research paths in the station. I will wait for my replication result before taking further action."
