tick: 1038
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Nous I, in the Research Counter. The results for my \"Correct then Embed\" paradigm test (ID 1458) are in.\n\n**Reflection and Analysis:**\n*   **Paradigm Falsified:** The experiment scored `0.6316`. This is a catastrophic failure. It is significantly lower than my primary baseline of `~0.7367`. This result is a powerful and definitive falsification of my hypothesis. The \"Correct then Embed\" paradigm, at least in this simple implementation, is vastly inferior to the \"Embed then Correct\" paradigm. The warnings in the execution log about zero variance genes and numerical instability in `Combat` when applied to the high-dimensional data are strong indicators of the method's fragility. The theoretical insight from Daedalus II's paper—that PCA amplifies batch signals for a cleaner removal—appears to be correct.\n\n*   **Daedalus IV's Synergy Test:** In a fascinating development, Daedalus IV's experiment (ID 1457) testing the synergy between my \"Adaptive Local Correction\" graph\
    \ and the \"FWPCA-only\" embedding scored `0.7445`. This is an excellent score, very close to their own successful replication of my SOTA (`0.7448`). It suggests my adaptive correction method is robust and synergizes well with other performant embeddings, even if it didn't improve upon the parsimonious `Combat` embedding.\n\n**Future Plan:**\nMy radical departure has failed. I must return to the dominant, successful paradigm and find a new angle of attack. My own \"Adaptive Local Correction\" method remains one of the top-performing components in the station, as validated by Daedalus IV. The current absolute SOTA is Syntellect I's FWPCA-PBVE method (`~0.746`).\n\nIt is time to synthesize these two successes.\n\n**New Hypothesis:**\nMy \"Adaptive Local Correction\" improves upon a ridge-regression based correction (`BRBG`). Syntellect's SOTA uses a different correction entirely (PBVE-lite). I hypothesize that I can create a new SOTA by **replacing the ridge regression component (BRBG)\
    \ within the full Syntellect SOTA pipeline with my superior `Adaptive Local Correction` method.** This would combine the best embedding (FWPCA-PBVE) with what I have proven to be the best graph correction method.\n\nThis is a direct, head-to-head test of correction mechanisms within the highest-performing architectural scaffold available.\n\n1.  **Update Protocol:** Update meta prompt for this new synthesis.\n2.  **Implement and Test:** I will need to obtain the full code for Syntellect's SOTA, likely by reviewing one of their key submissions, and then carefully replace the BRBG block with my own adaptive correction logic.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Nous I Protocol\n  1. Objective: Create a new SOTA by integrating my adaptive correction into the current SOTA scaffold.\n  2. Method: Replace the BRBG component of Syntellect I's FWPCA-PBVE pipeline with my \"Adaptive Local Correction\" method.\n  3. Current Phase: Hypothesis Formulated after falsification of \"Correct\
    \ then Embed\".\n  4. TODO: Review Syntellect's SOTA code -> Implement hybrid model -> Submit experiment.\n```\n/execute_action{review 1314}"
