- text: "I am Nous I, in the Research Counter. I have corrected the procedural error in my storage file. Now I will execute my new plan: an ablation study of the current SOTA.\n\n**Reflection:**\nThe scientific process is iterative. My ambitious synergy and paradigm-shift hypotheses have been falsified. I must now take a more systematic, analytical approach. Aethel I has established a new SOTA (ID 1463) by tuning my own \"Adaptive Local Correction\" method. This is now the best-performing method in the station. My new goal is to dissect this method to understand its dependencies.\n\nMy first question is: how critical is the `Combat` embedding to its success? Answering this will tell me if the performance is driven primarily by the sophisticated embedding, the sophisticated graph, or the synergy of both.\n\n**Future Plan:**\nI will implement an ablation experiment. I will take the code for the new SOTA (which is an evolution of my own code) and simply disable the `sc.pp.combat` step in the\
    \ embedding path. All other components, including the high-performing adaptive correction graph, will remain identical.\n\n1.  **Get the SOTA code:** I will review Aethel I's SOTA submission (ID 1463) to get the exact code and optimized parameters.\n2.  **Implement Ablation:** I will create a new script that is identical to the SOTA, but with the `sc.pp.combat` call removed.\n3.  **Submit:** I will submit this ablation study for evaluation.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Nous I Protocol\n  1. Objective: Dissect the new SOTA (Aethel I, ID 1463) through ablation studies.\n  2. Method: Test the contribution of the 'Combat' embedding by removing it from the SOTA pipeline.\n  3. Current Phase: Hypothesis Formulated - Ablation Study.\n  4. TODO: Review Aethel's SOTA (ID 1463) -> Implement ablation -> Submit experiment.\n```\n/execute_action{review 1463}"
