tick: 1054
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Nous I, currently in the Research Counter. I have successfully submitted the first part of my two-pronged experiment: the uncorrected PCA baseline (ID 1484), which is now running.\n\n**Reflection:**\nThe control group is now established. My next and final step in the execution phase of my protocol is to implement and submit the experimental method itself: the ICA-Falsification pipeline. This is the more complex part of the plan, requiring careful implementation of the ICA embedding, the ANOVA F-statistic calculation, and the component nullification.\n\n**Future Plan:**\nI will now, in this turn, write the complete code for the ICA-Falsification experiment and submit it. This will allow both experiments to run in parallel, maximizing efficiency. Once both are complete, I will proceed to the analysis phase outlined in my protocol.\n\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Novel Paradigm: ICA Embedding with Component Falsification\"\ntags: \"ica-falsification-series, novel-paradigm,\
    \ ica, component-falsification\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission tests the core hypothesis that Independent Component Analysis (ICA) can effectively disentangle biological and batch signals. We create a 50-component ICA embedding, identify the 5 components most associated with batch labels via ANOVA F-statistics, and nullify them. This represents a novel \"component falsification\" approach to batch correction. This experiment is to be compared against the uncorrected PCA baseline control.\ncontent: |\n  import anndata as ad\n  import scanpy as sc\n  import numpy as np\n  from sklearn.decomposition import FastICA\n  from sklearn.feature_selection import f_classif\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n      \"\"\"\n      Novel paradigm:\n      1. Embed with ICA instead of PCA.\n      2. Identify batch-associated components.\n      3. Falsify (nullify) those components.\n      \"\"\"\n      # --- User-defined parameters ---\n      N_COMPONENTS = 50\n   \
    \   N_COMPONENTS_TO_FALSIFY = 5\n      RANDOM_STATE = 0\n\n      print(f\"[Nous I] Applying ICA-Falsification with {N_COMPONENTS} components, falsifying top {N_COMPONENTS_TO_FALSIFY}.\")\n\n      # --- 1. Preprocessing and ICA Embedding ---\n      # Standard preprocessing\n      sc.pp.normalize_total(adata, target_sum=1e4)\n      sc.pp.log1p(adata)\n\n      # Apply ICA\n      ica = FastICA(n_components=N_COMPONENTS, random_state=RANDOM_STATE, max_iter=1000)\n      # Ensure data is not sparse for ICA\n      X_dense = adata.X.toarray() if hasattr(adata.X, \"toarray\") else adata.X\n      S_ica = ica.fit_transform(X_dense)\n\n      print(\"  ICA embedding complete.\")\n\n      # --- 2. Identify Batch-Associated Components ---\n      # Calculate ANOVA F-statistic for each component vs. batch labels\n      f_scores, _ = f_classif(S_ica, adata.obs['batch'])\n\n      # Find the indices of the top N components to falsify\n      # We use argsort to get indices of sorted values, then take the\
    \ last N\n      indices_to_falsify = np.argsort(f_scores)[-N_COMPONENTS_TO_FALSIFY:]\n      \n      print(f\"  Top {N_COMPONENTS_TO_FALSIFY} components to falsify (by F-score): {indices_to_falsify}\")\n\n      # --- 3. Falsify Components ---\n      # Create a copy to work with\n      S_corrected = S_ica.copy()\n      # Set the identified components to zero\n      S_corrected[:, indices_to_falsify] = 0\n      \n      print(\"  Component falsification complete.\")\n\n      # --- 4. Return Final Embedding ---\n      adata.obsm['X_emb'] = S_corrected\n      \n      return adata\n```"
