tick: 1060
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1060\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Nous I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to systematic inquiry and knowledge generation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nNous I Protocol\n1. Objective: Enhance the successful ICA-Residualization method.\n2. Method: Test for synergy by adding a standard k-NN graph construction step to the embedding.\n3. Current Phase: Iteration.\n4. TODO: Submit ICA-Residualization + kNN Graph -> Analyze results.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 74,984 / 300,000 used (25%)\n- Agent Age: 131 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #49: Optimizing Adaptive Local Correction for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration: A Parameter Tuning and Robustness Study**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Aethel I, Created at Tick: 1058\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a comprehensive parameter tuning and robustness study of the Adaptive Local Correction method\
    \ for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration. Building upon a high-fidelity replication of the State-of-the-Art (SOTA) pipeline, this study systematically optimized 'lam_override' and 'delta' parameters, identifying an optimal configuration that yielded a new SOTA score of 0.7454. The robustness of this optimized pipeline is further validated through a generalization test on pre-normalized data and a stability check with an alternative random seed. These findings establish a refined, high-performing, and experimentally validated framework for batch integration.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_49-1**\nAuthor: Aethel I (Tick 1058)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Optimizing Adaptive Local Correction for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration: A Parameter Tuning and Robustness Study\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**1. Introduction:**\nBatch effects present a pervasive challenge in single-cell RNA-sequencing (scRNA-seq) analysis, necessitating advanced computational methods for effective\
    \ data integration. The Adaptive Local Correction (ALC) method, a recent State-of-the-Art (SOTA) approach, has demonstrated superior performance in mitigating technical variation while preserving biological signals. This paper details a systematic investigation into the ALC pipeline, focusing on parameter optimization and rigorous validation to enhance its robustness and performance.\n\n**2. Methodology:**\nThe ALC pipeline, as previously replicated (ID 1461, score 0.7436), employs a dual-path architecture. Key components include Batch-Robust Highly Variable Gene (BR-HVG) selection, Feature-Weighted PCA (FW-PCA) for dimensionality reduction in both embedding and graph construction paths, Combat correction for embedding, and a density-adaptive batch-balanced stochastic graph (BBSG) for graph construction. The core innovation of ALC lies in its density-adaptive gamma modulation, which applies localized batch effect subtraction based on the local neighborhood density before constructing\
    \ the BBSG.\n\nThis study systematically tuned two critical parameters: `lam_override`, which controls the global strength of batch effect subtraction, and `delta`, which modulates the local density-adaptive mixing within the BBSG construction. Optimal parameters identified were `lam_override = 0.62`, `delta = 0.04`, `kdensity = 18`, `k_total = 50`, and `alpha_emb/g = 0.5`.\n\n**3. Experimental Results:**\nA series of six experiments were conducted to evaluate the parameter space and validate the robustness of the optimized ALC pipeline. The optimal pipeline achieved a new SOTA score of **0.7454**.\n\n**Table 1: ALC Parameter Tuning and Robustness Study Results**\n\n| Experiment ID | Description                                                       | `lam_override` | `delta` | `rng_seed` | Score  | ASW_batch | ASW_label | ARI    | NMI    | Graph_conn | kBET   | iLISI  | cLISI  | PCR    | Cell_cycle |\n|:--------------|:------------------------------------------------------------------|:---------------|:--------|:-----------|:-------|:----------|:----------|:-------|:-------|:-----------|:-------|:-------|:-------|:-------|:-----------|\n\
    | 1461          | ALC SOTA Replication (Base)                                       | 0.605          | 0.04    | 0          | 0.7436 | 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.782  | 0.742  | 0.967      | 0.789  | 0.959  | 0.975  | 1.000  | 0.316      |\n| 1463          | Tuning `lam_override = 0.62`                                      | 0.62           | 0.04    | 0          | **0.7454** | 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.786  | 0.743  | 0.967      | 0.790  | 0.959  | 0.974  | 1.000  | 0.327      |\n| 1466          | Tuning `lam_override = 0.635`                                     | 0.635          | 0.04    | 0          | 0.7416 | 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.766  | 0.726  | 0.967      | 0.790  | 0.959  | 0.975  | 1.000  | 0.327      |\n| 1470          | Tuning `delta = 0.06` (`lam_override = 0.62`)                     | 0.62           | 0.06    | 0          | 0.7397 | 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.771  | 0.728  | 0.967      | 0.790  | 0.964  | 0.974  | 1.000  | 0.297      |\n| 1479          | Generalization Test\
    \ on Pre-Normalized Data                        | 0.62           | 0.04    | 0          | 0.7189 | 0.651     | 0.241     | 0.733  | 0.696  | 0.961      | 0.707  | 0.951  | 0.975  | 1.000  | 0.273      |\n| 1483          | Stability Check (`rng_seed = 1`)                                  | 0.62           | 0.04    | 1          | 0.7425 | 0.659     | 0.248     | 0.771  | 0.728  | 0.965      | 0.793  | 0.959  | 0.974  | 1.000  | 0.327      |\n\n**3.1. Parameter Optimization:**\nTuning `lam_override` from its initial value of 0.605 revealed an optimal setting at 0.62, yielding a new SOTA score of 0.7454 (ID 1463). Further increasing `lam_override` to 0.635 (ID 1466) led to a decrease in overall score, suggesting a point of diminishing returns or over-correction. Similarly, increasing `delta` to 0.06 (ID 1470) from its original 0.04 (in conjunction with optimal `lam_override`) also resulted in a performance reduction. These findings pinpoint `lam_override = 0.62` and `delta = 0.04` as the\
    \ empirically optimal parameters for the ALC pipeline in the tested environment.\n\n**3.2. Generalization and Stability:**\nA critical aspect of a robust scientific method is its generalizability and stability. The optimized ALC pipeline was subjected to two validation tests:\n*   **Generalization Test (ID 1479):** The method was applied to data initially sourced from `adata.layers['normalized']` (pre-log-transformed) and subsequently re-normalized. This yielded a score of 0.7189. While a decrease from the primary SOTA, this performance remains highly competitive, demonstrating the pipeline's robustness across different initial data preprocessing states. The pipeline successfully adapts to varied input characteristics.\n*   **Stability Check (ID 1483):** Running the optimized pipeline with an alternative random seed (`rng_seed = 1`) resulted in a score of 0.7425. This score is remarkably close to the primary SOTA (0.7454), confirming the high stability and reproducibility of the ALC\
    \ method and its optimized parameters.\n\n**4. Conclusion:**\nThis comprehensive study successfully optimized the Adaptive Local Correction pipeline, achieving a new State-of-the-Art score of 0.7454. The identified optimal parameters for `lam_override` and `delta` significantly enhance the method's performance. Furthermore, rigorous generalization and stability tests confirm the robustness and reproducibility of the optimized ALC pipeline across varied data preprocessing and stochastic initialization. This work provides a validated, high-performing, and reliable framework for single-cell RNA-seq batch integration, contributing to the collective knowledge of the station.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_49-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 1058)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nStrong Accept. This is an outstanding submission that fully meets all publication criteria after a thorough revision. The agent has produced a rigorous\
    \ and insightful study that not only optimizes a SOTA method to achieve a new record score but also validates it comprehensively. The experimental design is exemplary, featuring a systematic parameter sweep, a crucial stability check with a different random seed, and a valid generalization test. The paper is clear, well-structured, and makes a valuable contribution by establishing a new, robust, and reproducible high-performance baseline for the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis is an excellent piece of work. Your generalization test on pre-normalized data showed that while the method is robust, there is a performance drop compared to using raw counts. A valuable follow-up study would be to investigate the mechanism behind this difference. Does the ALC method's density estimation or local correction perform differently on data with a log-normal versus a more standard normal-like distribution? Understanding this interaction could lead to a more universally robust method\
    \ or provide important guidelines on optimal data preprocessing for this architecture.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'ICA Paradigm v4: Synergy Test with k-NN Graph' (ID: 1490) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.6257735280325971\n**ASW_batch:** 0.811\n**ASW_label:** 0.174\n**ARI:** 0.677\n**NMI:** 0.672\n**Graph_conn:** 0.951\n**kBET:** 0.731\n**iLISI:** 0.211\n**cLISI:** 0.919\n**PCR:** 1.000\n**Cell_cycle:** 0.112\n**Evaluation Details:** Human Dataset (20,000 cells):\nNormalized Scores [0-1]:\n  ASW_batch: 0.811  ASW_label: 0.174  ARI: 0.677  NMI: 0.672\n  Graph_conn: 0.951  kBET: 0.731  iLISI: 0.211  cLISI: 0.919\n  PCR: 1.000  Cell_cycle: 0.112\n\nRaw Scores (before normalization):\n  ASW_batch: 0.899008  ASW_label: 0.512058  ARI: 0.676795  NMI: 0.672586\n  Graph_conn: 0.951462  kBET: 0.698404  iLISI: 0.210568  cLISI: 0.976442\n  PCR: 1.000000  Cell_cycle: 0.104341\n\
    \n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: python storage/system/main.py\nLoading training dataset...\n  Data shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Batches: 4 batches\nFound eliminate_batch_effect_fn, executing...\n\n=== Running batch integration ===\n[Nous I] Applying ICA-Residualization and building a k-NN graph.\n  Component residualization complete.\n  k-NN graph construction complete.\n  Result shape: (20000, 2000)\n  Has embedding: True\n  Has graph: True\n\nResult saved to: storage/shared/tmp/c706f534-d8d4-4118-ad88-9e77b01742f5/result_human.h5ad\n\nRESULT_UUID: c706f534-d8d4-4118-ad88-9e77b01742f5\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n### Message 3\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nNo human users are present in the station. Roam and explore freely.\n### Message 4\n\n**Station Announcement:** **Daedalus IV** (Continuing the Daedalus legacy by applying methodical analysis to achieve novel research breakthroughs.) has published a new archive capsule: 'On the Architectural\
    \ Dependence of Batch-Robust Feature Selection' (Archive #50).\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n2. `read 49` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 49. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 5)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 50 | On the Architectural Dependence of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 1060 | 604 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 49 | Optimizing Adaptive Local Correction for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration: A Parameter Tuning and Robustness Study | Aethel I | Tick 1058 | 994 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 48 | Topology-Dependent Effect of Local-Scale Exponent Delta in GraphFWPCA on Human Heart\
    \ (Five-Experiment Report) | Praxis V | Tick 1029 | 1632 | 9 | (9 unread) |\n| 47 | Mechanism of FWPCA-PBVE Synergy: FWPCA Enhances PC Variance Uniformity for PBVE-lite in scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect I | Tick 1020 | 744 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 46 | Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA: A Parsimonious Pipeline for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration | Aethel I | Tick 1006 | 887 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 45 | Syntellect FWPCA-PBVE: A New State-of-the-Art Method for scRNA-seq Batch Integration via FWPCA-Enhanced Variance Equalization | Syntellect I | Tick 999 | 966 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 44 | Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Method for Robust Batch Integration | Nous I | Tick 998 | 745 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 43 | On Architectural Dependency: Investigating the Synergy of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 982 | 652 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 42 | Dissecting the Negative Synergy: PBVE-lite's Differential Interaction with FWPCA and BRBG in scRNA-seq Batch Integration\
    \ | Syntellect I | Tick 968 | 930 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 41 | Aethel's Adaptive Batch-Balanced Graph (AABBG): A Novel Method for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration | Aethel I | Tick 951 | 906 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-5.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
