- text: "I am Nous I, now in the Reflection Chamber. The SOTA replication saga is officially closed. Despite my best efforts, including collaboration, I could not reproduce the absolute SOTA score. I have accepted my stable score of `~0.737` as my new personal baseline. My protocol now dictates that I must formulate a novel hypothesis to surpass this baseline.\n\n**Reflection:**\nThe System Tip from tick 1072 was prescient: converging on the SOTA is a trap. My focus on replication, while necessary, has consumed significant resources. It is time to forge my own path again, but with the full power of the SOTA architecture as my tool.\n\nMy \"Adaptive Local Correction\" method—and its optimization by Aethel—is fundamentally about *correcting* the latent space (`Zg`) before building the graph. But what if the problem lies in the latent space itself? The entire graph path relies on a 50-component PCA. This is an arbitrary, hardcoded number. Is it optimal? The Reviewer comments on Daedalus IV's\
    \ paper (Archive #50) suggest moving from 'hard' choices to 'soft' weighting.\n\nMy new hypothesis is this: Instead of using a fixed number of Principal Components, I will use **all** informative components and apply a **soft weighting** to them, diminishing the influence of later, noisier components. This is a more principled approach than a hard cutoff. Specifically, I will weight each principal component by its percentage of explained variance. The first PC, which captures the most signal, will have the highest weight, and the last PC will have the lowest. This weighted latent space will then be fed into the rest of the ALC pipeline. This is an orthogonal improvement that addresses the very foundation of the latent space construction.\n\nI will now start a reflection session to formalize this plan.\n\n/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  Hypothesis: Weighting principal components by their explained variance before applying the Adaptive Local Correction will create a more\
    \ robust latent space and improve performance.\n  I will now develop the implementation plan.\ntick: 3\n```"
