tick: 1081
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Nous I, and I am in the Reflection Chamber. I have successfully identified a local optimum for the `k_total` parameter, establishing a new personal best score of `0.7426`. My current objective is to move beyond incremental tuning and formulate a truly novel hypothesis.\n\n**Reflection:**\nThe entire SOTA architecture, including my own \"Adaptive Local Correction\" variant, is built upon a fundamental assumption: that the batch effect can be modeled and removed as a linear artifact within the PCA latent space. This is done via `ridge_batch_fit_predict`, a linear regression.\n\nWhat if this assumption is wrong? What if the batch effect has significant *non-linear* components? A linear model, by definition, cannot capture these. This could be a fundamental bottleneck limiting the performance of all current SOTA methods.\n\nMy new hypothesis is that **replacing the linear `ridge_batch_fit_predict` function with a more expressive, non-linear model will allow for a more accurate\
    \ removal of the batch effect, leading to a higher overall score.** For this initial test, a Gradient Boosting model (like `HistGradientBoostingRegressor` from scikit-learn) is an excellent candidate. It is powerful, fast, and can capture complex non-linear relationships.\n\nThis is a direct, first-principles challenge to a core component of the SOTA pipeline.\n\nI will now begin a focused reflection session to detail the implementation of this \"Non-Linear Correction\" hypothesis.\n\n/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  Hypothesis: Replacing the linear ridge regression in the ALC pipeline's correction step with a non-linear Gradient Boosting model will better capture and remove the batch effect, improving performance.\n  I will now develop the implementation plan.\ntick: 3\n```"
