tick: 1081
role: model
parts:
- text: "**Implementation Plan - Step 1: Core Logic Modification**\n\nThe modification will be a surgical replacement within my best-performing script (the one that scored `0.7426` with `k_total=40`). The target is the `_ridge_batch_fit_predict` function and its invocation.\n\n1.  **Create a New Correction Function:** I will define a new function, `_nonlinear_batch_fit_predict`, to encapsulate the Gradient Boosting logic. This function will have a similar signature to the original, taking the one-hot encoded batch matrix `B` and the latent space `Zg` as input.\n\n2.  **Implement the Non-Linear Model:**\n    *   Inside this new function, I will use `sklearn.ensemble.HistGradientBoostingRegressor`. This model is chosen for its speed and effectiveness on tabular data.\n    *   The model needs to predict each of the 50 principal components independently. A simple and effective way to do this is to train one model per component. I will loop from `j=0` to `49`:\n        *   For each `j`, I will\
    \ instantiate a new `HistGradientBoostingRegressor`. I will use default parameters for the initial experiment to keep it simple and test the core concept. A `random_state` will be set for reproducibility.\n        *   I will fit this model to predict the `j`-th column of `Zg` (i.e., `Zg[:, j]`) using `B` as the feature matrix.\n        *   I will then use the trained model to predict the values for `Zg[:, j]`, storing the result in the `j`-th column of a new `Zhat` matrix.\n    *   The function will return the fully populated `Zhat` matrix, which now represents the *non-linear* prediction of the batch effect.\n\n3.  **Replace the Function Call:** In the main `eliminate_batch_effect_fn`, I will replace the line `Zhat = _ridge_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg, l2=l2)` with `Zhat = _nonlinear_batch_fit_predict(B, Zg)`.\n\nAll other parts of the pipeline—the preprocessing, FWPCA, the calculation of `R2` and `gamma`, the adaptive local correction, and the DAQB graph construction—will remain absolutely\
    \ identical. This ensures that any change in score is directly attributable to the change from a linear to a non-linear correction model. This is a clean and powerful test of the hypothesis."
