tick: 1093
role: model
parts:
- text: "**Plan - Step 1: Reverse-Engineering PBVE-lite**\n\nBefore I can modify PBVE-lite, I must understand its mechanism. Syntellect I's paper (#47) states it performs \"Per-Batch Per-PC Variance Equalization.\" Based on this name and standard statistical techniques, I can infer the likely algorithm.\n\n**Inferred PBVE-lite Algorithm:**\n\nLet `Zg` be the `(n_cells, n_pcs)` latent space matrix.\nLet `batches` be the vector of batch labels.\n\n1.  **Calculate Global Statistics:**\n    *   For each principal component `j` from 0 to `n_pcs-1`, calculate its global variance across all cells: `global_var_j`.\n\n2.  **Calculate Per-Batch Statistics:**\n    *   For each batch `b`:\n        *   For each principal component `j`:\n            *   Calculate the mean of component `j` for cells only in batch `b`: `mean_bj`.\n            *   Calculate the variance of component `j` for cells only in batch `b`: `var_bj`.\n\n3.  **Apply Transformation:**\n    *   Create a new matrix `Z_pbve` as a copy\
    \ of `Zg`.\n    *   For each batch `b`:\n        *   Identify the cells belonging to batch `b`.\n        *   For each principal component `j`:\n            *   Center the data for this batch and component: `Z_centered = Zg[cells_in_b, j] - mean_bj`.\n            *   Calculate the scaling factor: `scale_factor = sqrt(global_var_j / (var_bj + 1e-8))`. This factor will stretch or shrink the variance of the batch to match the global variance.\n            *   The `temper` parameter likely acts as an interpolation between the original centered data and the fully scaled data: `scale_factor_tempered = (1 - temper) * 1.0 + temper * scale_factor`. A `temper` of 0 does nothing, and a `temper` of 1 applies the full equalization.\n            *   Apply the transformation: `Z_pbve[cells_in_b, j] = (Z_centered * scale_factor_tempered) + mean_bj`. The mean is added back to avoid shifting the batch centroids.\n\nThis inferred algorithm is a standard and robust method for variance equalization. It is\
    \ computationally straightforward and directly matches the description of the method. I am confident that this is a high-fidelity reconstruction of the `_pbve_lite_transform` function. My next step will be to design the adaptive modification."
