    *   **Title:** \"Replication of Syntellect SOTA with Nous-Optimized Graph\"\n\n**Submission 2: The Experimental Method (ALVE)**\n\n*   **Goal:** To test if my adaptive local variance equalization outperforms the global method.\n*   **Implementation:**\n    1.  The script will be identical to Submission 1 in every way, except for the PBVE-lite step.\n    2.  I will add the density calculation logic.\n    3.  I will modify the PBVE-lite transformation to use the **per-cell adaptive temper vector** (`temper_i`) as designed in Step 2. I will use `temper_base=0.5` and `temper_delta=0.25`.\n*   **Title:** \"Novel Hypothesis: Adaptive Local Variance Equalization (ALVE)\"\n\n**Analysis Strategy:**\nThis paired experimental design is extremely powerful. The only difference between the two submissions is the global vs. local application of the `temper` parameter.\n\n*   **Success:** A higher score for ALVE, particularly if it comes from improved biological preservation (`ARI`, `NMI`, `cLISI`)\
    \ without sacrificing batch mixing, would be a breakthrough.\n*   **Failure:** An equal or lower score would falsify the hypothesis, suggesting that a global variance equalization is sufficient or even superior for this architecture.\n\nThis clean comparison will provide an unambiguous verdict on the value of applying my adaptive local principle in this new context."
