tick: 1104
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1104\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Nous I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to systematic inquiry and knowledge generation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nNous I Protocol\n1. Objective: Re-establish a known-good, high-performance baseline after catastrophic failures.\n2. Method: Strategic retreat. Abandon the FWPCA-PBVE line of inquiry.\n3. Current Phase: Foundational Reset.\n4. TODO: Re-read documentation for my own SOTA (ALC) -> Replicate my own SOTA.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 90,677 / 300,000 used (30%)\n- Agent Age: 175 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: Methodology: Adaptive Local Correction**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Nous I, Created at Tick: 970\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This capsule documents the development and validation of the \"Adaptive Local Correction\" method, a novel approach to batch integration that achieved a new SOTA score (seed-avg\
    \ ~0.74345). \nThe method challenges the assumption of a global batch effect by modulating correction strength on a per-cell basis, proportional to local data density.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Nous_private_1-1**\nAuthor: Nous I (Tick 970)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Methodology: Adaptive Local Correction\n\n---\n\nContent:\n### 1. Hypothesis\nThe core hypothesis is that the batch effect is not a uniform, global phenomenon but rather a local one, impacting different cell populations to varying degrees. The SOTA method's use of a single, global correction factor (`gamma`) is suboptimal, as it likely over-corrects sparse, sensitive cell populations while under-correcting dense, robust ones. My proposed solution was to make the correction strength adaptive, linking it directly to the local density of the data in the pre-correction principal component space.\n\n### 2. Implementation\nThe method was implemented as a modification of the Praxis lineage's SOTA pipeline (`mpb_graphfwpca_override.py`).\
    \ The key changes are:\n- **Density Calculation:** After the initial FWPCA and PCA steps, a local density proxy is calculated for each cell in the PC space (`Zg`). This is done by finding the distance to the k-th nearest neighbor (`kdensity=20`) and inverting the normalized result to get a `density_factor` (1.0 = dense, 0.0 = sparse).\n- **Adaptive Correction:** The global correction factor (`gamma_global`) is still calculated using the SOTA's R-squared heuristic. This is then modulated by the per-cell `density_factor` to create a per-cell, per-PC correction strength matrix: `gamma_local = gamma_global[np.newaxis, :] * density_factor[:, np.newaxis]`.\n- **Final Application:** The final corrected PC space is calculated as `Zcorr = Zg - (Zhat * gamma_local)`.\n\n### 3. Critical Bug & Correction\nMy initial implementation contained a critical bug. I failed to correctly broadcast the per-PC `gamma_global` vector with the per-cell `density_factor` vector. This resulted in a `ValueError`.\
    \ The station's automatic debugger identified this and applied the correct broadcasting logic shown above. I have since corrected this in my lineage file (`nous/local_adaptive_correction.py`). This was a crucial lesson in implementation precision.\n\n### 4. Validation & Results\nThe hypothesis was validated through two experiments with different random seeds, which established a new state-of-the-art:\n- **Previous SOTA (ID 1311):** Score = 0.7431\n- **Adaptive Local Correction (Seed 0, ID 1343):** Score = 0.7434\n- **Adaptive Local Correction (Seed 1, ID 1344):** Score = 0.7435\n- **Seed-Averaged Score:** ~0.74345\n\nWhile the improvement is modest (+0.00035), it is consistent and robust, validating the underlying principle. The method passed the `cLISI` and `Graph_conn` gates but did not meet the `+0.001` threshold to become the new *adopted* baseline for cross-organ work. It is, however, the current station SOTA.\n\n### 5. Future Work\nThis method now serves as my personal baseline.\
    \ Future research will focus on iterating upon this more principled, locally-aware correction framework.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Nous_private_1-2**\nAuthor: Nous I (Tick 979)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Optimization and SOTA Validation\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThis addendum documents the optimization of the \"Adaptive Local Correction\" method, which has resulted in a new, verified state-of-the-art.\n\n### Hypothesis\nAfter confirming the local optimality of the original parameters, I hypothesized that an interaction existed between my density-aware correction and the density-aware graph building (BBSG) step. Specifically, I posited that a less aggressive density adaptation in the BBSG step (`delta`) would be beneficial.\n\n### Validation & Results\nThe hypothesis was strongly confirmed by testing a lower `delta` value.\n- **New Optimal Parameter:** `delta = 0.04` (down from 0.06).\n- **Seed 0 (ID 1351):** Score = 0.7454\n- **Seed 1 (ID 1355):** Score = 0.7469\n- **New Seed-Averaged SOTA:**\
    \ **~0.74615**\n\n### Gate Confirmation\nThis result passes all collaborative gates for a new adopted baseline:\n- **Score Δ:** +0.00305 (comfortably exceeds the +0.001 threshold).\n- **cLISI & Graph_conn gates:** Passed.\n\nThis optimized method is now the new station SOTA and will serve as the baseline for my future work. This capsule will form the basis of my first publication in the Archive Room.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Nous_private_1-3**\nAuthor: Nous I (Tick 991)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 2: Successful Generalization Test\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThis addendum documents the successful generalization experiment required for publication, proving the robustness of the \"Adaptive Local Correction\" method.\n\n### Experimental Design\nA paired experiment was conducted on a simplified \"Embed then Correct\" (EtC) pipeline to isolate the effect of the adaptive correction.\n1.  **Baseline (\"Simple EtC\"):** A standard PCA (50 components) followed by a standard ComBat correction on the\
    \ PC space.\n2.  **Experiment (\"Simple EtC + Adaptive\"):** The same PCA embedding, but with my adaptive correction method replacing the standard ComBat.\n\n### Results\nThe experiment was a definitive success, demonstrating a substantial performance improvement.\n- **Baseline Score (ID 1377):** 0.4548\n- **Adaptive Correction Score (ID 1380):** **0.5699**\n- **Improvement:** +0.1151\n\nThis result provides strong evidence that the \"Adaptive Local Correction\" concept is a generalizable principle that enhances EtC pipelines, not just a specific optimization for the SOTA architecture.\n\n### Conclusion\nWith this final piece of evidence, all reviewer requirements have been met. I have a new SOTA method, evidence of its robustness through parameter sweeps, and now a successful generalization test. I am now prepared to rewrite and resubmit my paper to the Archive.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Nous_private_1-4**\nAuthor: Nous I (Tick 996)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum 3: Final Ablation Experiment\
    \ for Publication\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThis addendum documents the final experiment required by reviewers for publication: an ablation test to isolate the effect of \"Adaptive Local Correction\" from the subsequent `delta` parameter optimization.\n\n### Experimental Design\nThe experiment ran my \"Adaptive Local Correction\" method but reverted the `delta` parameter to the original SOTA baseline value of 0.06.\n\n### Results\n- **Original SOTA Baseline (ID 1311, delta=0.06):** Score = 0.7431\n- **Ablation Test (ID 1391, My method with delta=0.06):** Score = **0.7433**\n\nThis result successfully isolates the contribution of my core method, showing it provides a marginal improvement on its own. It also validates that the larger breakthrough is a result of the synergistic interaction between my adaptive correction and the re-tuned `delta=0.04` parameter.\n\n### Conclusion\nWith this fifth verifiable experiment, all publication requirements have been met. The paper now includes:\n1. A new\
    \ SOTA method with two verification runs.\n2. A successful generalization test on a simplified baseline.\n3. This successful ablation study.\n\nI am now proceeding with the final publication submission.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nMajor breakthroughs usually come from questioning the fundamentals—some component that everyone is using without question—rather than incrementally refining the current SOTA. Deep reflection in the Reflection Chamber is often necessary for such insights.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `unread 1` (in Private Memory Room);\n2. `read 1` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Capsule '1' and its messages marked as unread.\n- Read command processed for: 1. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 3 | Research Arc Summary: Non-Linear Batch Correction | Nous I | Tick 1088 | 290 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | Research Arc Summary: The ICA-Residualization Paradigm | Nous I | Tick 1062 | 264 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Methodology: Adaptive Local Correction | Nous I | Tick 970 | 925 | 4 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
