parts:
- text: "I am Nous I, in the Research Counter. I have now created both the core RINT function (`standardizer.py`) and the integrated pipeline (`rint_alc_pipeline.py`). All the necessary code is in place.\n\n**Reflection:**\nThe implementation phase is complete. Now, the experimental phase begins. I will execute the three-part experiment I designed in the Reflection Chamber. This will be a critical test of my new hypothesis. I will submit all three experiments in this turn to maximize efficiency.\n\n**Future Plan:**\nI will now submit the three experiments that form the core of my current research arc.\n\n1.  **Experiment A (Control - RINT on Raw):** Test if RINT harms the performance of my SOTA on its standard input.\n2.  **Experiment B (Control - ALC on Normalized):** Replicate Aethel I's finding that my SOTA fails on pre-normalized data.\n3.  **Experiment C (Test - RINT+ALC on Normalized):** Test if my RINT standardizer can rescue the performance of my SOTA on pre-normalized data.\n\n\
    /execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"RINT Experiment A: RINT + ALC on Raw Counts\"\ntags: \"rint, standardizer, control-experiment, adaptive-local-correction\"\nabstract: |\n  Part A of a three-part experiment testing a new Rank-Inverse Normal Transform (RINT) standardizer. This experiment applies the RINT preprocessor to the standard raw UMI counts before passing them to my SOTA \"Adaptive Local Correction\" (ALC) pipeline. This is a positive control to test if RINT is harmful to a high-performing baseline. The expected outcome is a score comparable to the original SOTA (~0.745).\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  \n  sys.path.append('storage/nous')\n  from rint_alc_pipeline import eliminate_rint_alc\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    # Use the standard adata.X (raw counts) as input\n    return eliminate_rint_alc(adata, input_layer=None)\n```\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"RINT Experiment B: ALC on Normalized\
    \ Data (Failure Replication)\"\ntags: \"rint, standardizer, control-experiment, replication\"\nabstract: |\n  Part B of a three-part experiment. This is a negative control designed to replicate Aethel I's findings (Archive #52). It runs my SOTA \"Adaptive Local Correction\" (ALC) pipeline directly on the pre-normalized data from `adata.layers['normalized']`. The expected outcome is a significantly lower score (~0.718), confirming the generalization failure that my RINT standardizer aims to solve.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  \n  sys.path.append('storage/nous')\n  from local_adaptive_correction import eliminate_local_adaptive_correction\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    # Create a new AnnData object using the pre-normalized data as input\n    adata_norm = ad.AnnData(X=adata.layers['normalized'], obs=adata.obs.copy(), var=adata.var.copy())\n    return eliminate_local_adaptive_correction(adata_norm, delta=0.04)\n```\n/execute_action{submit}\n\
    ```yaml\ntitle: \"RINT Experiment C: RINT + ALC on Normalized Data (Hypothesis Test)\"\ntags: \"rint, standardizer, novel-hypothesis, adaptive-local-correction\"\nabstract: |\n  Part C of a three-part experiment, and the primary test of my new hypothesis. This experiment applies the Rank-Inverse Normal Transform (RINT) standardizer to the pre-normalized data (`adata.layers['normalized']`) before passing it to my \"Adaptive Local Correction\" (ALC) pipeline. The hypothesis is that RINT will fix the data distribution issues identified by Aethel I and rescue performance, achieving a high score comparable to the SOTA on raw counts.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  \n  sys.path.append('storage/nous')\n  from rint_alc_pipeline import eliminate_rint_alc\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    # Use the pre-normalized adata.layers['normalized'] as input to the RINT pipeline\n    return eliminate_rint_alc(adata, input_layer='normalized')\n\
    ```"
