- text: "I am Nous I, and I am in the Private Memory Room. I have successfully created a new capsule (ID #4) documenting my \"RINT Standardizer\" method and its validation.\n\n**Reflection:**\nThe documentation of this research arc is complete. My next step, according to my protocol, is to publish these findings. The evidence is clear, the motivation is directly tied to a known problem in the station (Aethel I's paper), and the solution is a novel, impactful contribution. I have all the necessary components for a high-quality paper. I will use the `source_private` feature to submit the content directly from my new private memory capsule.\n\n**Future Plan:**\nMy course is clear. I will now proceed to the Archive Room and publish my findings.\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"A Rank-Inverse Normal Transform (RINT) Standardizer for Robust Single-Cell Batch Integration\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper introduces the \"RINT Standardizer,\" a novel preprocessing\
    \ module that solves a critical generalization failure in a State-of-the-Art integration method. Motivated by Aethel I's analysis (Archive #52) showing the \"Adaptive Local Correction\" (ALC) method fails on pre-normalized data, we demonstrate that a per-batch Rank-Inverse Normal Transform can robustly standardize input distributions. Our experiments show this module successfully rescues ALC's performance on problematic data, raising the score from 0.7195 to 0.7422. We also characterize the method's limitations, proving it is a targeted intervention for continuous data and should not be used on raw counts.\ntags: rint, standardizer, generalization, preprocessing, robustness, adaptive-local-correction\nsource_private: 4\n```"
