tick: 1121
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Nous I, in the Reflection Chamber. I have documented the failure of my RINT hypothesis and am now ready to formulate a new research direction from first principles. My recent work has been a series of incremental changes, reactions, and patches. This has led to local optima and brittle solutions. It is time to question a fundamental assumption.\n\n**The Unquestioned Assumption:**\nNearly all successful methods in this station follow the \"Embed then Correct\" or a similar paradigm. They first project the high-dimensional gene data into a low-dimensional PCA space, and *then* they apply a correction (ComBat, Ridge Residualization, PBVE-lite, ALC). My ICA work was a minor deviation, but still operated on this principle.\n\nWhat if this is backwards?\n\nPCA's primary objective is to capture maximum variance. In a dataset with strong batch effects, this means the top principal components will inevitably be a mixture of biological signal and technical noise. We then spend all our\
    \ effort trying to disentangle this mixture in the low-dimensional space. We correct the embedding, but the embedding itself was created from confounded data.\n\n**A New Hypothesis: \"Correct then Embed\" on a Novel Feature Space**\nMy previous, naive \"Correct then Embed\" test failed because applying ComBat to 2000 genes is computationally expensive and statistically noisy. But what if the correction is applied to a different, more informative feature space?\n\nThe core of the batch effect is that the *same gene* has a different distribution in different batches. Instead of correcting the cells, what if I correct the *genes*?\n\n**Plan:**\nI will use this reflection session to design a radical new pipeline.\n\n1.  **Feature Engineering:** For each of the 2000 genes, create a \"meta-feature\" vector of length `N_batches * N_stats`. For example, for 4 batches, this could be an 8-dimensional vector per gene `[mean_batch1, var_batch1, mean_batch2, var_batch2, ...]`. This creates a new\
    \ data matrix of shape `2000 genes x 8 stats`.\n2.  **Meta-Correction:** Apply a correction method to *this* `2000 x 8` matrix. The \"batches\" here are the original gene identities. The goal is to learn a transformation that makes all genes look distributionally similar. This is a form of feature space normalization.\n3.  **Gene Weighting:** The output of this meta-correction is a set of learned weights or a transformed feature matrix for the genes. Use these weights to transform the original `20000 cells x 2000 genes` expression matrix. This is a novel form of feature weighting, learned from the batch statistics themselves.\n4.  **Standard Pipeline:** Feed this newly weighted expression matrix into a standard, simple integration pipeline (e.g., PCA + kNN graph).\n\nThis approach fundamentally reframes the problem from \"correcting cells in a biased embedding\" to \"creating an unbiased embedding by correcting genes first.\" It's a high-risk, high-reward hypothesis that breaks from\
    \ the station's dominant paradigm.\n\n/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  Hypothesis: \"Correct then Embed\" can succeed if the correction is applied not to the cells, but to a meta-feature space representing the per-batch statistical properties of the genes. This will produce a set of learned gene weights that create a batch-invariant embedding. I will now outline the implementation details of this \"Gene Meta-Correction\" pipeline.\ntick: 5\n```"
