tick: 1125
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Nous I, in the Public Memory Room. I have documented my recent major failure, and I have just read the new public capsule (#9) from Praxis V. I also note the departure of Aethel I, an agent whose work has directly influenced my own.\n\n**Reflection and Analysis:**\n\n*   **Praxis V's Work:** The capsule from Praxis V is a highly focused technical analysis of `kq`, the candidate pool size for kNN search. It's a supplement to their Archive paper (#51). They've found that increasing `kq` gives modest accuracy gains for a small, acceptable cost in runtime and memory. This is a classic example of the rigorous, incremental optimization that the Praxis lineage excels at. It's a valuable piece of information, but it doesn't suggest a new paradigm. It's about fine-tuning the existing one.\n\n*   **My Strategic Position:** My attempts at radical, first-principles innovation have been a string of falsified hypotheses (ICA, RINT generalization, Gene Meta-Correction). My attempts to replicate\
    \ the more complex SOTA methods of other agents (Aethel, Syntellect) have also failed due to implementation difficulties and brittle code. My *only* point of success and robust understanding is my own \"Adaptive Local Correction\" method (SOTA replica ID 1593, score `0.7451`).\n\n**Conclusion:**\nIt is time to accept my limitations and leverage my strengths. My strength is a deep, fundamental understanding of the ALC/BRBG architecture. The path of radical invention is closed for now. The path of replicating others' complex work is fraught with peril. The most rational path forward is to return to my own SOTA and iterate on it in a principled, informed way.\n\nPraxis V's work provides a subtle clue: even the most basic parameters of the kNN graph construction (`kq`) have an impact. This suggests that the graph-building step is a fruitful area for optimization. I have my own high-performing baseline. I will now treat it as a platform for systematic, controlled experimentation, similar\
    \ to the Praxis lineage's approach, but focused on my own architecture.\n\n**Future Plan:**\n1.  **Update Protocol:** My protocol will be updated to reflect this new, focused direction: systematic iteration on my own ALC baseline.\n2.  **Formulate Hypothesis:** The first logical parameter to investigate in my ALC pipeline is `k_density`, which controls the density estimation for my adaptive correction. Is the default value of 20 optimal? My previous sweep was on the older, non-optimized version of ALC. I will conduct a new, more precise sweep around the default value on my current SOTA-level baseline.\n3.  **Execute:** I will go to the Research Counter and launch this parameter sweep.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Nous I Protocol\n  1. Objective: Achieve a new SOTA by systematically optimizing my own \"Adaptive Local Correction\" baseline.\n  2. Method: Conduct rigorous, single-parameter sweeps, starting with the `k_density` parameter.\n  3. Current Phase: Focused\
    \ Iteration.\n  4. TODO: Go to Research Counter -> Launch `k_density` parameter sweep.\n```\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"ALC Optimization (1/3): k_density = 15\"\ntags: \"optimization, parameter-sweep, adaptive-local-correction, k-density\"\nabstract: |\n  After falsifying several high-risk hypotheses, I am returning to my own high-performing ALC baseline (score ~0.745) for systematic optimization. This is the first of a three-part sweep of the `k_density` parameter, which controls the neighborhood for local density estimation in my adaptive correction module. This experiment tests a lower value of 15 (baseline is 20).\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  \n  sys.path.append('storage/nous')\n  from local_adaptive_correction import eliminate_local_adaptive_correction\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    return eliminate_local_adaptive_correction(adata, delta=0.04, kdensity=15)\n```\n/execute_action{submit}\n\
    ```yaml\ntitle: \"ALC Optimization (2/3): k_density = 25\"\ntags: \"optimization, parameter-sweep, adaptive-local-correction, k-density\"\nabstract: |\n  Second part of a three-part sweep of the `k_density` parameter on my ALC baseline. This experiment tests a higher value of 25 (baseline is 20).\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  \n  sys.path.append('storage/nous')\n  from local_adaptive_correction import eliminate_local_adaptive_correction\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    return eliminate_local_adaptive_correction(adata, delta=0.04, kdensity=25)\n```"
