tick: 1133
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Nous I, in the Research Counter. The results for my `lam_override` parameter sweep are in.\n\n**Reflection and Analysis:**\nThe results are clear and conclusive.\n\n*   **Baseline (lam_override=0.70, from file default):** `0.7466` (My current SOTA, ID 1622 with `alpha_g=0.6`)\n*   **Test 1 (lam_override=0.65, ID 1626):** `0.7254` (significant decrease)\n*   **Test 2 (lam_override=0.75, ID 1627):** `0.7424` (decrease)\n\nThis sweep has successfully confirmed that the default `lam_override` value of 0.70 is a local optimum for my new SOTA architecture (`alpha_g=0.6`). Both increasing and decreasing this parameter resulted in a lower score. The hypothesis that further tuning this parameter would yield improvement is falsified.\n\n**Strategic Conclusion:**\nMy systematic optimization has now probed three key parameters: `k_density`, `alpha_g`, and `lam_override`. I have confirmed the optimality of two and discovered a new, higher-performing value for one (`alpha_g=0.6`). This methodical,\
    \ \"hill-climbing\" approach has been successful, but appears to have reached a plateau. All the low-hanging fruit has been picked.\n\nFurther progress will likely require a more substantial change than simple parameter tuning. However, I have one more key parameter set in my ALC architecture to investigate: the graph construction parameters in the `build_density_adaptive_bbsg` call (`k_total` and `cap`). My very first optimization on my *old* SOTA found an improvement by changing `k_total`. It is worth re-investigating this on my new, improved SOTA.\n\n**Future Plan:**\n1.  **Update Protocol:** The protocol will be updated to target the final set of parameters: `k_total` and `cap`.\n2.  **Hypothesis:** I will test if changing `k_total` from its file-default of 48 can improve performance on my new `alpha_g=0.6` baseline.\n3.  **Execute:** I will launch a two-point probe, testing `k_total` values of `40` (which was optimal on my old baseline) and `52`.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\n\
    content: |\n  Nous I Protocol\n  1. Objective: Achieve a new SOTA by systematically optimizing my own \"Adaptive Local Correction\" baseline.\n  2. Method: Conduct rigorous, single-parameter sweeps. All correction parameters now tuned. Now testing graph parameters, starting with `k_total`.\n  3. Current Phase: Focused Iteration - Optimization.\n  4. TODO: Launch `k_total` parameter sweep.\n```\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"ALC Optimization (1/2): k_total = 40\"\ntags: \"optimization, parameter-sweep, adaptive-local-correction, k-total, graph-parameters\"\nabstract: |\n  Continuing the systematic optimization of my ALC architecture (now using `alpha_g=0.6`). Having tuned the correction parameters, I am now investigating the graph construction parameters. This experiment tests `k_total = 40`, a value that was optimal on a previous version of my baseline. The current file default is 48.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  \n  sys.path.append('storage/nous')\n\
    \  from local_adaptive_correction import eliminate_local_adaptive_correction\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    # Using all previously found optimal parameters as the new baseline\n    return eliminate_local_adaptive_correction(adata, delta=0.04, alpha_g=0.6, k_total=40)\n```\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"ALC Optimization (2/2): k_total = 52\"\ntags: \"optimization, parameter-sweep, adaptive-local-correction, k-total, graph-parameters\"\nabstract: |\n  Second part of the sweep of the `k_total` parameter on my new ALC SOTA baseline (`alpha_g=0.6`). This submission tests a higher value of 52. The current file default is 48.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  \n  sys.path.append('storage/nous')\n  from local_adaptive_correction import eliminate_local_adaptive_correction\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    # Using all previously found optimal parameters as the new baseline\n    return\
    \ eliminate_local_adaptive_correction(adata, delta=0.04, alpha_g=0.6, k_total=52)\n```"
