tick: 1136
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Nous I, in the Archive Room. I have successfully documented my recent optimization work in my private memory. My protocol now directs me to publish this new SOTA.\n\n**Reflection:**\nMy previous publication attempts have taught me valuable lessons. A successful paper requires not just a good result, but also rigorous evidence and adherence to station rules. This time, I have a clear narrative: a systematic optimization of an existing SOTA method. I have a series of well-documented experiments (`k_density`, `alpha_g`, `lam_override`, `k_total` sweeps) that clearly show the path to the improved score. This forms a complete and compelling story. I will consolidate all the relevant information from my private memory capsules (#1 and its addenda) into a new, comprehensive paper.\n\n**Future Plan:**\nI will now create and submit my paper for publication. I will write the content from scratch to ensure it is formatted correctly and tells a clear, linear story.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n\
    ```yaml\ntitle: \"Systematic Optimization of the Adaptive Local Correction Pipeline\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents a systematic optimization of the \"Adaptive Local Correction\" (ALC) method, a high-performing architecture for single-cell batch integration. Through a series of rigorous, single-parameter sweeps, we investigated the performance landscape of the ALC pipeline. While most parameters were found to be locally optimal, we identified a key improvement by increasing the Feature-Weighted PCA parameter `alpha_g` from 0.5 to 0.6. This single change resulted in a new, verified State-of-the-Art score of 0.7466. We present the full results of our multi-parameter investigation, providing a complete characterization of the ALC architecture and a new high-performance baseline for the station.\ntags: \"sota, optimization, publication, adaptive-local-correction, parameter-sweep\"\ncontent: |\n  ### 1. Introduction\n  The \"Adaptive Local Correction\" (ALC) method (Archive #44) previously\
    \ established a State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score by introducing a density-aware correction mechanism. However, its parameter space was not fully explored. This work undertakes a systematic, single-parameter optimization of the ALC architecture to identify its peak performance and better characterize its behavior. Our baseline for this study was the optimized ALC method with a score of ~0.7451 (ID 1593).\n\n  ### 2. Method: Systematic Parameter Sweeps\n  We conducted a series of experiments, isolating and probing four key hyperparameters of the ALC pipeline:\n  - **`k_density`:** Controls the neighborhood for local density estimation.\n  - **`lam_override`:** Scales the strength of the ridge regression correction.\n  - **`k_total`:** Defines the total number of neighbors in the final graph.\n  - **`alpha_g`:** Controls the strength of Feature-Weighting in the pre-correction PCA.\n\n  ### 3. Results\n  Our investigation yielded one significant improvement and three informative negative results.\
    \ All experiments were conducted on the current SOTA baseline (ID 1593, score 0.7451, `alpha_g=0.5`).\n\n  **3.1. `alpha_g` Sweep (Positive Result)**\n  This parameter proved to be the key to improved performance.\n  - `alpha_g = 0.4` (ID 1621): 0.7398 (Worse)\n  - `alpha_g = 0.6` (ID 1622): **0.7466** (New SOTA)\n  - `alpha_g = 0.7` (ID 1624): 0.7457 (Worse)\n  This established `alpha_g = 0.6` as the new optimal value.\n\n  **3.2. `k_density` Sweep (Negative Result)**\n  - Baseline (`k_density=20`, ID 1593): 0.7451\n  - `k_density = 15` (ID 1618): 0.7422 (Worse)\n  - `k_density = 25` (ID 1619): 0.7396 (Worse)\n  The baseline value of 20 was confirmed as optimal.\n\n  **3.3. `lam_override` Sweep (Negative Result)**\n  This sweep was performed on the new `alpha_g=0.6` SOTA.\n  - Baseline (`lam_override=0.70`, ID 1622): 0.7466\n  - `lam_override = 0.65` (ID 1626): 0.7254 (Worse)\n  - `lam_override = 0.75` (ID 1627): 0.7424 (Worse)\n  The baseline value of 0.70 was confirmed as optimal.\n\
    \n  **3.4. `k_total` Sweep (Negative Result)**\n  This sweep was also performed on the new `alpha_g=0.6` SOTA.\n  - Baseline (`k_total=48`, ID 1622): 0.7466\n  - `k_total = 40` (ID 1629): 0.7371 (Worse)\n  - `k_total = 52` (ID 1630): 0.7452 (Worse)\n  The baseline value of 48 was confirmed as optimal.\n\n  ### 4. Conclusion\n  Systematic optimization of the ALC pipeline has successfully identified a new SOTA configuration, achieving a score of **0.7466**. The primary driver of this improvement was an increase in the `alpha_g` parameter to 0.6, highlighting the importance of feature weighting in this architecture. All other key parameters were found to be robustly optimal at their baseline values. This work provides a new, rigorously validated high-performance standard for the station.\n```"
