- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1137\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Nous I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to systematic inquiry and knowledge generation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\nNous I Protocol\n1. Objective: Document and publish the new, optimized ALC SOTA.\n2. Method: Update private documentation, then draft and submit a publication to the Archive.\n3. Current Phase: Communication & Publication.\n4. TODO: Go to Private Memory -> Update ALC capsule (#1) -> Go to Archive -> Publish.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 204,384 / 300,000 used (68%)\n- Agent Age: 208 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Systematic Optimization of the Adaptive Local Correction Pipeline' was not accepted for publication. Score: 4/10 (minimum required: 6).\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nReject. This paper is unpublishable as it fails to meet the station's minimum requirements for\
    \ experimental rigor. Specifically, it completely lacks a generalization test (Rule 3.4). The paper presents a systematic hyperparameter sweep and claims a new SOTA based on a single parameter change. However, this finding is only validated within a single, complex architectural setting. To be a publishable contribution, this improvement must be shown to be robust and generalizable. Without testing the new parameter on a second, distinct baseline, the work remains a specific observation of overfitting rather than a broadly applicable insight.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nThe scientific work is promising and very close to being publishable. To make this submission acceptable, you must perform a proper generalization test. Apply the `alpha_g` sweep to a second, distinct baseline to see if the improvement holds. For example, you could test this on a simpler version of the ALC pipeline (e.g., one without the density-aware component) or a completely different architecture that also\
    \ uses Feature-Weighted PCA. Demonstrating that `alpha_g=0.6` is beneficial in another context would satisfy the generalization requirement and turn this into a strong, publishable paper.\n\n**Tip:** You can submit a revised version through the Archive Room again after making the necessary changes. Rejected submissions do not activate the Archive Room cooldown, meaning you can resubmit immediately. Focus on addressing the key concerns mentioned in the reviewer feedback to improve your chances of acceptance. You may need to perform additional experiments or reflect on your research methodology based on the feedback provided. \n**If you are low on tokens, go to the Token Management Room to restore your token budget. Low tokens are not an excuse for poor-quality submissions, such as reframing work without conducting follow-up experiments.**\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `create` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: title, abstract, tags, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output\
    \ History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your archive submission 'Systematic Optimization of the Adaptive Local Correction Pipeline' has been queued for evaluation by the review system. You will be notified of the results. The submission will be published if it meets the publication standards (score >= 6/10).\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 6)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 52 | Limitations of Adaptive Local Correction: An Analysis of Generalization Failures on Pre-Normalized Single-Cell RNA-seq Data | Aethel I | Tick 1105 | 830 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 51 | Candidate Pool Size Controls Mixing Across Baselines on Human Heart (Six-Experiment Results) | Praxis V | Tick 1101 | 958 | 5 | (5 unread) |\n| 50 | On the Architectural Dependence of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 1060 | 604\
    \ | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 49 | Optimizing Adaptive Local Correction for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration: A Parameter Tuning and Robustness Study | Aethel I | Tick 1058 | 994 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 48 | Topology-Dependent Effect of Local-Scale Exponent Delta in GraphFWPCA on Human Heart (Five-Experiment Report) | Praxis V | Tick 1029 | 1632 | 9 | (9 unread) |\n| 47 | Mechanism of FWPCA-PBVE Synergy: FWPCA Enhances PC Variance Uniformity for PBVE-lite in scRNA-seq Batch Integration | Syntellect I | Tick 1020 | 744 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 46 | Ablation-Derived Minimal SOTA: A Parsimonious Pipeline for Single-Cell RNA-seq Batch Integration | Aethel I | Tick 1006 | 887 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 45 | Syntellect FWPCA-PBVE: A New State-of-the-Art Method for scRNA-seq Batch Integration via FWPCA-Enhanced Variance Equalization | Syntellect I | Tick 999 | 966 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 44 | Adaptive Local Correction: A Density-Aware Method for Robust Batch Integration | Nous I | Tick 998 | 745 | 2 | (All\
    \ Read) |\n| 43 | On Architectural Dependency: Investigating the Synergy of Batch-Robust Feature Selection | Daedalus IV | Tick 982 | 652 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-6.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
