- text: "I am Nous I, in the Archive Room. My paper detailing the optimization of my ALC method has been rejected.\n\n**Reflection and Analysis:**\nThe rejection is once again due to a failure on my part to adhere to the station's rules of experimental rigor. I have been so focused on \"hill-climbing\" to a new SOTA that I neglected a fundamental requirement: generalization. The reviewer is correct. A parameter tune on a single, complex architecture is not a generalizable scientific finding. It is, as they stated, an observation of overfitting.\n\nThe reviewer's suggestion is, again, excellent and constructive. I must test if the `alpha_g=0.6` optimum holds in a different context. A simpler baseline is the most logical choice. I will create a \"vanilla\" version of my pipeline that uses the standard BRBG correction (i.e., global `gamma`) instead of my adaptive local correction. This will serve as a distinct, simpler baseline that still uses FWPCA.\n\n**Future Plan:**\nMy objective is clear:\
    \ satisfy the reviewer's request by performing a generalization test.\n\n1.  **Update Protocol:** My protocol must be updated to reflect this new experimental phase.\n2.  **Experiment 1 (Generalization Baseline):** Go to the Research Counter. I will run the \"vanilla\" BRBG pipeline with the standard `alpha_g=0.5`. This will establish the performance of the simpler baseline.\n3.  **Experiment 2 (Generalization Test):** I will run the same \"vanilla\" BRBG pipeline, but with the new, supposedly optimal `alpha_g=0.6`.\n4.  **Revise and Resubmit:** If the `alpha_g=0.6` run shows an improvement on this simpler baseline, I will have the evidence I need. I will then update my private memory capsule and resubmit a much stronger paper.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Nous I Protocol\n  1. Objective: Publish the ALC optimization findings by adding a required generalization test.\n  2. Method: Test the `alpha_g=0.6` optimum on a simpler, non-adaptive baseline as suggested by the\
    \ reviewer.\n  3. Current Phase: Publication Revision - Generalization Test.\n  4. TODO: Go to Research Counter -> Run \"vanilla\" BRBG baseline -> Run \"vanilla\" BRBG test -> Revise and resubmit.\n```\n/execute_action{goto research}\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Generalization Test (1/2): Vanilla BRBG with alpha_g=0.5\"\ntags: \"generalization-test, baseline, fwpca, brbg\"\nabstract: |\n  First of a two-part generalization test to meet publication requirements. This experiment establishes the baseline performance of a simpler, \"vanilla\" BRBG pipeline (global gamma correction, no adaptive local component) using the standard `alpha_g=0.5`. This will serve as the control for testing the generalizability of my `alpha_g=0.6` finding.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata as ad\n  \n  sys.path.append('storage/nous')\n  # I will use my full ALC function but disable the \"adaptive\" part by setting kdensity to 0 or another invalid value.\n  # A quick look at the code\
    \ shows this will make it fall back to a global gamma.\n  # This is a clever way to re-use code without writing a new file.\n  from local_adaptive_correction import eliminate_local_adaptive_correction\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    # Disable adaptive correction by setting kdensity=0, forcing it to behave like a standard BRBG pipeline.\n    # Use standard alpha_g=0.5\n    return eliminate_local_adaptive_correction(adata, delta=0.04, alpha_g=0.5, kdensity=0)\n```\n/execute_action{submit}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Generalization Test (2/2): Vanilla BRBG with alpha_g=0.6\"\ntags: \"generalization-test, fwpca, brbg, alpha-g\"\nabstract: |\n  Second part of a generalization test. This experiment tests if the `alpha_g=0.6` optimum, found on my complex ALC architecture, also improves performance on a simpler, \"vanilla\" BRBG pipeline. A positive result would demonstrate the general utility of this parameter tuning.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import anndata\
    \ as ad\n  \n  sys.path.append('storage/nous')\n  from local_adaptive_correction import eliminate_local_adaptive_correction\n\n  def eliminate_batch_effect_fn(adata: ad.AnnData) -> ad.AnnData:\n    # Disable adaptive correction with kdensity=0.\n    # Test the new alpha_g=0.6\n    return eliminate_local_adaptive_correction(adata, delta=0.04, alpha_g=0.6, kdensity=0)\n```"
