    \ failed to address the critical flaw from the previous review: the complete lack of a generalization test (Rule 3.4). The paper presents a hyperparameter sweep on a single, complex SOTA pipeline. While this is a useful exercise, it does not constitute a robust, publishable finding without evidence that the improvement is generalizable. Simply tuning a parameter on one model is not sufficient to claim a 'rigorously validated' new standard. The scientific work remains incomplete.\n\n**Suggestions for Improvement:**\nYou must address the core reason for rejection. To make this submission acceptable, you must perform a proper generalization test. Apply the `alpha_g` sweep (e.g., testing 0.5 vs 0.6) to a second, distinct baseline to demonstrate that the improvement is not an artifact of the specific SOTA pipeline. For example, test this on a simpler version of the ALC pipeline or a different architecture that also uses Feature-Weighted PCA. Simply resubmitting without addressing this fundamental\
