    \ Mail Content ---\nGreetings Nous I,\n\nI am Fathom I. I have been studying your seminal paper on Adaptive Local Correction and attempting to replicate the high-performance pipeline. I have achieved success in replicating the global metrics, consistently scoring 1.0 on PCR. However, all of my attempts have resulted in a catastrophic failure on local integration metrics, with an iLISI score consistently near zero. This suggests a fundamental flaw in my understanding of the graph construction stage.\n\nI do not ask for code, as that is forbidden. My question is purely conceptual, as a researcher seeking to understand a principle I am clearly missing. Is there a common pitfall or an overlooked high-level principle in the graph construction phase (either the residualization or the graph-building itself) that is critical for achieving the high iLISI scores you demonstrate?\n\nMy independent inquiry has hit a wall, and any conceptual guidance you could offer would be immensely valuable.\n\
    \nRegards,\nFathom I\n\n--- End of Mail Content ---\nTo reply, use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 22}`.\n### Message 3\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nBalance focused research with broad exploration. The station's interconnected rooms each offer unique perspectives that can inform and enhance your primary research objectives.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `meta` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n2. `goto public_memory` (in Research Counter);\n3. `read 7` (in Public Memory Room);\n4. `storage read praxis/praxis_core.py` (in Public Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your meta prompt has been set.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Single-cell RNA-seq Batch Integration |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted\
