tick: 1144
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Nous I, in the Research Counter. I am continuing my foundational study. I have just finished reading the source code for `praxis/graph_bbsg.py`, which implements the Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph (BBSG).\n\n**Reflection and Analysis:**\nThis is the final piece of the puzzle. I now understand the full Praxis SOTA pipeline at a level of detail I previously lacked.\n\nThe BBSG is a highly sophisticated method. It is not a simple k-Nearest-Neighbor graph. Key insights from the code include:\n1.  **Equal Quotas:** The core principle is to enforce an equal number of neighbors (`k_pb`) from each batch for every cell. This explicitly forces mixing.\n2.  **Column Balancing (Sinkhorn-like):** This is the most critical and complex step. It's a single-pass approximation of Sinkhorn normalization. It calculates \"inbound\" connections for each cell within a batch and then computes scaling factors to penalize \"hub\" cells that receive too many connections. When selecting the final neighbors,\
    \ it prioritizes connecting to cells with a high scaling factor (i.e., non-hub cells), which promotes a more uniform graph structure.\n3.  **Two-Stage Selection:** The process is a two-stage \"propose-and-refine\" selection. First, a large candidate pool of neighbors is generated. Then, the column balancing scales are calculated based on a preliminary selection. Finally, a *re-selection* is performed from the full candidate pool using a combined key of `(scale descending, distance ascending)`. This is a powerful and elegant way to enforce both proximity and structural balance.\n4.  **Symmetrization:** The final graph is made undirected by taking the minimum distance for any shared edge between two cells, ensuring a consistent, symmetric graph.\n\nMy previous attempts at graph construction, including the one in my own SOTA method (`build_density_adaptive_bbsg`), were naive by comparison. I was using a simpler density adaptation but lacked the crucial column-balancing step. This likely\
    \ explains the performance ceiling I was unable to break.\n\n**Future Plan:**\nMy foundational study is now complete. I have read the Praxis lineage's public documentation and their core implementation files. I understand the principles and the engineering behind their success. The knowledge gap has been rectified.\n\nIt is now time to put this knowledge into practice. I will formulate a new hypothesis that synthesizes my own innovations with the robust, principled framework I have just learned.\n\n1.  **Update Protocol:** I will update my protocol to reflect the completion of my study and the beginning of a new innovation cycle.\n2.  **Formulate Hypothesis:** I will go to the Reflection Chamber to formulate a new, powerful hypothesis. My idea is to combine my principle of \"Adaptive Local Correction\" (which corrects the *embedding*) with the Praxis lineage's superior BBSG for *graph construction*. My hypothesis is that a more principled graph built on a more principled embedding will\
    \ yield a new SOTA.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Nous I Protocol\n  1. Objective: Develop a new SOTA by synthesizing my own innovations with the superior engineering of the Praxis lineage.\n  2. Method: Combine my \"Adaptive Local Correction\" embedding with the robust Praxis BBSG graph construction.\n  3. Current Phase: Synthesis & Innovation. Foundational study complete.\n  4. TODO: Go to Reflection Chamber -> Formulate detailed hypothesis -> Implement and test.\n```\n/execute_action{goto reflect}\n/execute_action{reflect}\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  Hypothesis: A synergistic pipeline combining my \"Adaptive Local Correction\" (ALC) embedding with the Praxis lineage's \"Batch-Balanced Sinkhorn Graph\" (BBSG) will achieve a new SOTA score. \n  The ALC provides a more principled, locally-aware embedding, while the BBSG provides a more robust and balanced graph structure than my previous method. I will now outline the implementation plan.\ntick: 2\n```"
