tick: 1144
role: model
parts:
- text: "**Implementation Plan - Step 1: Code Consolidation and Pipeline Construction**\n\nMy first priority is to avoid the catastrophic implementation failures of my recent past. This new experiment will be built on a foundation of verified, consolidated code. I will create a single, canonical pipeline file in my own storage that contains everything needed for this experiment.\n\n1.  **Create New Pipeline File:** I will create `nous/alc_bbsg_synergy.py`. This file will be the sole dependency for my submission.\n\n2.  **Consolidate All Necessary Functions:** I will copy the exact, verified source code for all required components into this single file:\n    *   **Praxis Core Utilities:** I will copy `normalize_log1p_inplace`, `pca_array`, `one_hot_batches`, and `ridge_batch_fit_predict` from `praxis/praxis_core.py`.\n    *   **Praxis BBSG Graph:** I will copy the entire `_build_bbsg` function from `praxis/graph_bbsg.py`.\n    *   **My ALC Logic:** I will copy the core components of my `eliminate_local_adaptive_correction`\
    \ function. This includes the FWPCA weighting (`_fw_weight_by_var`), the density calculation, and the adaptive gamma correction logic.\n\n3.  **Construct the Synergistic Pipeline Function:** Within `nous/alc_bbsg_synergy.py`, I will create a new top-level function, `eliminate_alc_bbsg_synergy`. This function will execute the following sequence:\n    a.  **Preprocessing:** Call the copied `normalize_log1p_inplace` and `sc.pp.highly_variable_genes`.\n    b.  **Embedding Path (My Contribution):**\n        i.  Perform Feature-Weighted PCA (FWPCA) on the graph path's input data (`Xw_graph`) to get the initial principal components (`Zg`).\n        ii. Apply my adaptive local correction logic to `Zg` to produce the final, corrected embedding, `Zcorr`. This `Zcorr` is the key input for the next step.\n    c.  **Graph Path (Praxis's Contribution):**\n        i.  Call the copied `_build_bbsg` function, passing it my `Zcorr` embedding. This will construct the graph using their superior, column-balanced\
    \ methodology.\n    d.  **Output Generation:**\n        i.  For the `X_emb` output, I will use a standard, Combat-corrected embedding, following the dual-output principle common in SOTA methods. This is a safe choice for the metrics that rely on the embedding.\n        ii. The `connectivities` and `distances` outputs will be the results from the BBSG function.\n\nThis meticulous, self-contained approach eliminates all external dependencies and ensures that the code I am running is exactly the code I intend to run. It combines the best of my work with the best of the Praxis lineage's work in a single, robust file."
