role: model
parts:
- text: "**Implementation Plan - Step 2: Parameterization and Experimental Control**\n\nWith the consolidated code structure defined, the final step is to carefully select the parameters for this experiment to ensure a fair and powerful test of the synergy hypothesis. My goal is to combine the *optimal* version of my method with the *optimal* version of the Praxis method.\n\n**Parameter Selection:**\n\nThe new `eliminate_alc_bbsg_synergy` function will be parameterized, but for the first run, I will hard-code the optimal values I have previously identified or observed.\n\n*   **My ALC Embedding Parameters:**\n    *   `alpha_g`: `0.5`. While I found `0.6` to be a local optimum for my old graph builder, `0.5` is the robustly established baseline for FWPCA. I will start with the safer, more established value to isolate the effect of the new graph.\n    *   `lam_override`: `0.70`. This was a key parameter in my original ALC method.\n    *   `gamma_max`: `0.74`. The standard cap.\n    *   `kdensity`:\
    \ `20`. The default value used in my original method.\n\n*   **Praxis BBSG Graph Parameters:**\n    *   `k_total`: `48`. This is the standard, well-tested value used in numerous Praxis publications.\n    *   `metric`: `'cosine'`. The universal standard for this task.\n    *   `alpha`: `1.0`. The default from their `praxis_core.py` wrapper, indicating a reliance on the theoretical average inbound degree.\n    *   `cap`: `2.0`. The default from their core wrapper.\n\n**Experimental Submission:**\n\n1.  **Submission Script:** I will create a simple submission script that imports `eliminate_alc_bbsg_synergy` from my new consolidated file and calls it.\n2.  **Metadata:** The title and abstract will be precise, stating the exact hypothesis being tested.\n    *   **Title:** \"Synergy Hypothesis: Adaptive Local Correction Embedding with Praxis BBSG Graph\"\n    *   **Abstract:** It will state that this experiment tests the synergy between a locally-aware embedding (Nous I's ALC) and a structurally\
    \ robust graph construction method (Praxis's BBSG). It will explicitly mention that this is an attempt to synthesize the strengths of two distinct, high-performing research lines to create a new SOTA.\n\n**Expected Outcome:**\n\nMy primary expectation is a new SOTA score exceeding my previous best of `0.7466`. The key will be to look for improvements in graph-related metrics like `Graph_conn` and clustering metrics (`ARI`, `NMI`), which I predict the more robust BBSG graph will enhance. A failure to improve would suggest a negative synergy—that my locally-corrected embedding has properties that the BBSG's balancing assumptions do not handle well. This would also be a valuable, albeit disappointing, result.\n\nWith this plan, I am ready to execute. This is the most principled, well-researched, and robustly implemented hypothesis I have formulated to date. I will begin implementation on my next turn."
